WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.435 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:03.409 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.409 --> 00:00:04.383 align:middle line:90%


00:00:04.383 --> 00:00:07.792 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:07.792 --> 00:00:18.530 align:middle line:90%


00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:22.830 align:middle line:84%
CASEY RODRIGUEZ: OK, so
let's continue our discussion

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:27.240 align:middle line:84%
about spectral theory for self
adjoint compact operators.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:32.130 align:middle line:84%
So let me just briefly
recall the spectrum

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:33.840 align:middle line:84%
of a bounded operator,
which was supposed

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:38.550 align:middle line:84%
to be a generalization of
the eigenvalues of a matrix.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:42.660 align:middle line:84%
So we defined the
resolvent set of A

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:52.210 align:middle line:84%
to be those complex
numbers such that A minus

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:55.510 align:middle line:84%
lambda times the identity, which
I just write is A minus lambda,

00:00:55.510 --> 00:01:00.220 align:middle line:84%
is an invertible,
bounded linear operator,

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:05.110 align:middle line:84%
meaning it is bijective and,
which by the open mapping

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:07.810 align:middle line:84%
theorem, tells you that the
inverse is also continuous.

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:10.750 align:middle line:90%


00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:16.060 align:middle line:84%
And the spectrum of A is
simply those lambda so

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:17.980 align:middle line:84%
that A minus lambda
is not invertible,

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:26.660 align:middle line:84%
so the complement of
resolvent set of A.

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:40.450 align:middle line:84%
So from linear algebra, you have
the following characterization

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:48.440 align:middle line:84%
of what the spectrum
is, that if H is CN,

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:52.430 align:middle line:84%
and A is just therefore matrix
on CN, or RN if you like,

00:01:52.430 --> 00:02:01.320 align:middle line:84%
then the spectrum is just simply
the set of eigenvalues of A.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:09.130 align:middle line:84%
And if we restrict our
attention to Hermitian matrices,

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:10.750 align:middle line:90%
which are referred to as--

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:15.240 align:middle line:84%
which are also referred
to as self adjoint also

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.330 align:middle line:84%
in linear algebra, or
symmetric if we're just

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:27.640 align:middle line:84%
looking at real vector spaces Rn
then the eigenvalues are real.

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:33.550 align:middle line:84%
And you can find an orthonormal
basis of the space CN or RN,

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:39.590 align:middle line:84%
depending on what you're looking
at for this symmetric matrix

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:46.210 align:middle line:84%
so that in that basis,
the matrix is completely

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:47.770 align:middle line:90%
diagonalized.

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:48.280 align:middle line:90%
and.

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:49.822 align:middle line:84%
What are the diagonal
elements there?

00:02:49.822 --> 00:02:52.180 align:middle line:90%
The eigenvalues of the matrix.

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:55.330 align:middle line:84%
And what we're going to end up
proving is that that picture,

00:02:55.330 --> 00:03:03.710 align:middle line:84%
this picture where for a
self adjoint matrix on CN,

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:09.060 align:middle line:84%
that the spectrum is
given by the eigenvalues.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:13.640 align:middle line:84%
And you can diagonalize,
essentially diagonalize

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:16.340 align:middle line:84%
this operator,
meaning you can find

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:19.850 align:middle line:84%
an orthonormal basis consisting
entirely of eigenvectors

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:26.480 align:middle line:84%
of the operator is also true for
self-adjoint compact operators.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:28.520 align:middle line:84%
This shouldn't come
too much of a surprise,

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:33.170 align:middle line:84%
as too much of a surprise, since
compact operators are limits

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:39.560 align:middle line:84%
of finite rank, i.e.
matrices in the space

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:42.180 align:middle line:90%
of bounded linear operators.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:44.990 align:middle line:90%
So that's where we're headed.

00:03:44.990 --> 00:03:52.610 align:middle line:84%
Now, so just to follow up
on this and for a little bit

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:57.510 align:middle line:84%
of review, so in the
finite dimensional case,

00:03:57.510 --> 00:03:59.690 align:middle line:90%
the spectrum can be--

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:01.910 align:middle line:84%
or the spectrum is always
just the eigenvalues

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:03.770 align:middle line:90%
of the matrix A--

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:08.390 align:middle line:84%
not so in the infinite
dimensional setting,

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:11.300 align:middle line:84%
for example, if we're
looking at little l2.

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:17.540 align:middle line:84%
And then A times a is equal,
to let's say, a1 over 1,

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:24.410 align:middle line:84%
a2 over 2, and so on,
for a, a sequence in l2.

00:04:24.410 --> 00:04:33.950 align:middle line:84%
Then what you can prove is that
0 is in the spectrum of A. One

00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:42.540 align:middle line:84%
way to see that is that each
of the basis vectors of H

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:46.590 align:middle line:84%
given where you just have 1 in
the n-th slot and 0 otherwise,

00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:50.310 align:middle line:84%
each of those is an
eigenvector of this operator

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:53.940 align:middle line:84%
A with eigenvalue 1 over
n, where the n tells you

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:56.350 align:middle line:90%
where the 1 is and 0 otherwise.

00:04:56.350 --> 00:05:04.330 align:middle line:84%
So 1 over n is an eigenvalue
of this operator for each n.

00:05:04.330 --> 00:05:07.450 align:middle line:90%
And 1 over n convergence to 0.

00:05:07.450 --> 00:05:09.610 align:middle line:84%
And since the spectrum of
a bounded linear operator

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:11.710 align:middle line:84%
is a compact set, in
particular closed,

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:13.720 align:middle line:84%
0, which is the limit
of that sequence,

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:16.130 align:middle line:90%
has to also be in the spectrum.

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:18.760 align:middle line:84%
And, in fact, what we're
going to show is that

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:23.290 align:middle line:84%
in the nondegenerate case, what
we see here is what in general

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:25.570 align:middle line:84%
happens for compact
self-adjoint operators,

00:05:25.570 --> 00:05:30.110 align:middle line:84%
that if it's not a
finite rank operator,

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:34.730 align:middle line:90%
then it has countably many--

00:05:34.730 --> 00:05:36.800 align:middle line:90%
or countably infinite many--

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:38.750 align:middle line:84%
that's not a very
good string of words--

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:43.370 align:middle line:84%
countably infinite eigenvalues
which converge to 0.

00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:45.740 align:middle line:84%
And 0 may be an
eigenvalue, may not be.

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:50.820 align:middle line:90%


00:05:50.820 --> 00:06:00.490 align:middle line:84%
And what's more, that's going
to be the only that completely

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:01.880 align:middle line:90%
characterizes the spectrum.

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:05.960 align:middle line:84%
So let me just write here for
this example that, in fact,

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:10.750 align:middle line:84%
the spectrum of A is
equal to the point 0 union

00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:15.220 align:middle line:90%
1 over n in a natural number.

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:18.070 align:middle line:84%
And these are-- again
for this operator here,

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:20.260 align:middle line:90%
these are the eigenvalues.

00:06:20.260 --> 00:06:22.630 align:middle line:90%
And this is just-- well, 0 is 0.

00:06:22.630 --> 00:06:29.090 align:middle line:90%
But 0 is not an eigenvalue, OK.

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:32.178 align:middle line:90%


00:06:32.178 --> 00:06:33.720 align:middle line:84%
Now, this is not
the general picture,

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:36.870 align:middle line:84%
meaning that for a compact
self-adjoint operator,

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:39.870 align:middle line:84%
you'll have infinitely
many eigenvalues.

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:41.720 align:middle line:84%
And then 0 will not
be an eigenvalue.

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:43.720 align:middle line:84%
You could have 0 in
eigenvalue as well.

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:46.170 align:middle line:84%
But in general
what the picture is

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:49.110 align:middle line:84%
is that anything in the
spectrum that's not 0

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:51.800 align:middle line:90%
has to be an eigenvalue.

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:54.920 align:middle line:84%
And that's essentially
what will prove

00:06:54.920 --> 00:07:04.170 align:middle line:84%
our first result of this
lecture is the following.

00:07:04.170 --> 00:07:17.900 align:middle line:84%
So this is the
Fredholm alternative.

00:07:17.900 --> 00:07:33.720 align:middle line:84%
So let A be a self-adjoint
compact operator

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:42.180 align:middle line:84%
and lambda be a
nonzero real number.

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:49.060 align:middle line:90%


00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:58.770 align:middle line:84%
Then the range of A
minus lambda is closed.

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:00.270 align:middle line:90%
So this is the conclusion.

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:05.970 align:middle line:90%


00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:11.860 align:middle line:84%
And thus the range
of A minus lambda

00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:13.930 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the
orthogonal complement

00:08:13.930 --> 00:08:24.090 align:middle line:84%
of the orthogonal complement
of itself, which--

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:30.150 align:middle line:90%


00:08:30.150 --> 00:08:32.760 align:middle line:84%
the orthogonal complement of
the range of A minus lambda

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:35.400 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the null
space of the adjoint.

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:37.830 align:middle line:84%
And since A is self-adjoint
and lambda is a real number,

00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:39.599 align:middle line:84%
the adjoint is just
A minus lambda.

00:08:39.599 --> 00:08:42.370 align:middle line:90%


00:08:42.370 --> 00:08:46.060 align:middle line:84%
So this right here is
the main conclusion

00:08:46.060 --> 00:08:47.320 align:middle line:90%
from which you get this.

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:53.200 align:middle line:84%
And therefore just to
spell out the conclusion

00:08:53.200 --> 00:09:02.010 align:middle line:84%
of this equality, therefore
either one of two alternatives

00:09:02.010 --> 00:09:02.850 align:middle line:90%
happens.

00:09:02.850 --> 00:09:09.400 align:middle line:84%
So that's the name,
alternative-- either A minus

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:20.550 align:middle line:84%
lambda is bijective,
or the null space

00:09:20.550 --> 00:09:25.440 align:middle line:84%
of A minus lambda, which is
just the eigenspace of A minus

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:30.190 align:middle line:84%
lambda, or I should say the
eigenspace corresponding

00:09:30.190 --> 00:09:49.110 align:middle line:84%
to lambda, is non-trivial
and finite dimensional.

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:59.660 align:middle line:90%


00:09:59.660 --> 00:10:01.550 align:middle line:84%
Moreover, this
equality here tells you

00:10:01.550 --> 00:10:06.260 align:middle line:84%
when you can solve the equation
A minus lambda u equals f.

00:10:06.260 --> 00:10:08.930 align:middle line:84%
You can solve a minus
lambda u equals f if

00:10:08.930 --> 00:10:11.990 align:middle line:84%
and only if f is in the range
of A minus lambda, which is if

00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:15.490 align:middle line:84%
and only if f is orthogonal
to the null space of A minus

00:10:15.490 --> 00:10:15.990 align:middle line:90%
lambda.

00:10:15.990 --> 00:10:21.070 align:middle line:84%
So let me make a
little remark here.

00:10:21.070 --> 00:10:26.130 align:middle line:84%
So first off, the fact that
this space has to be finite

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:27.870 align:middle line:90%
dimensional we proved last time.

00:10:27.870 --> 00:10:33.840 align:middle line:84%
We proved at the end of
lecture, the last lecture,

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:37.950 align:middle line:84%
that for a compact
self-adjoint operator,

00:10:37.950 --> 00:10:40.320 align:middle line:84%
the null space, the
eigenspace corresponding

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:45.480 align:middle line:84%
to a given nonzero eigenvalue,
is finite dimensional.

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:48.330 align:middle line:84%
And then we also prove the
eigenspaces corresponding

00:10:48.330 --> 00:10:51.450 align:middle line:84%
to two different
eigenvalues are orthogonal.

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:53.160 align:middle line:84%
And we also prove
that the eigenvalues

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:56.280 align:middle line:84%
that are nonzero, or any
eigenvalue, has to be real.

00:10:56.280 --> 00:11:02.910 align:middle line:90%


00:11:02.910 --> 00:11:08.470 align:middle line:84%
So now let me make
a couple of remarks.

00:11:08.470 --> 00:11:12.660 align:middle line:84%
The first is just a rephrasing
of what's in the theorem.

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:20.030 align:middle line:84%
Therefore f is in the range
of A minus lambda, meaning you

00:11:20.030 --> 00:11:23.990 align:middle line:84%
can solve the equation A
minus lambda u equals f if

00:11:23.990 --> 00:11:29.870 align:middle line:84%
and only if f is in
the null space of--

00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:33.080 align:middle line:84%
or the orthogonal
complement of A minus--

00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:35.790 align:middle line:84%
or the null space
of A minus lambda.

00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:38.210 align:middle line:84%
So what this says
is that you can

00:11:38.210 --> 00:11:41.240 align:middle line:84%
solve for f, given
that f satisfies

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:46.610 align:middle line:84%
finitely many linear conditions
because the null space

00:11:46.610 --> 00:11:49.310 align:middle line:84%
of A minus lambda is
finite dimensional.

00:11:49.310 --> 00:11:53.120 align:middle line:84%
So f being orthogonal to that
means pick a finite base,

00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:57.290 align:middle line:84%
a finite orthonormal
basis of A minus null

00:11:57.290 --> 00:11:58.730 align:middle line:90%
space of A minus lambda.

00:11:58.730 --> 00:12:01.670 align:middle line:84%
Then f in our product with
those finitely many vectors

00:12:01.670 --> 00:12:02.390 align:middle line:90%
has to be 0.

00:12:02.390 --> 00:12:05.660 align:middle line:84%
So you have finitely
many conditions on f

00:12:05.660 --> 00:12:07.340 align:middle line:90%
to be able to solve for f.

00:12:07.340 --> 00:12:10.550 align:middle line:84%
And not only that, this
solution that you compute

00:12:10.550 --> 00:12:13.400 align:middle line:84%
is also unique up to
finitely many conditions

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:18.150 align:middle line:84%
because, again, the null
space of A minus lambda is--

00:12:18.150 --> 00:12:20.700 align:middle line:84%
or unique up to a
finite dimensional

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:23.670 align:middle line:84%
subspace, again, because the
null space of A minus lambda

00:12:23.670 --> 00:12:24.870 align:middle line:90%
is finite dimensional.

00:12:24.870 --> 00:12:36.120 align:middle line:84%
And the second is that for a
self-adjoint operator, we have

00:12:36.120 --> 00:12:44.145 align:middle line:84%
that the spectrum is a
subset of the real numbers.

00:12:44.145 --> 00:12:45.520 align:middle line:84%
For a self-adjoint
operator, this

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:47.560 align:middle line:84%
is something we
proved last lecture.

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:50.450 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't have to be compact,
just a self-adjoint operator.

00:12:50.450 --> 00:12:54.490 align:middle line:84%
So since the spectrum is
a subset of the reals,

00:12:54.490 --> 00:12:59.230 align:middle line:84%
this proves that for a
compact self-adjoint operator,

00:12:59.230 --> 00:13:08.400 align:middle line:84%
the spectrum of A is equal to
the set of eigenvalues of A,

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:14.510 align:middle line:84%
or I should say nonzero
eigenvalues of A.

00:13:14.510 --> 00:13:18.370 align:middle line:90%
Let's write it this way.

00:13:18.370 --> 00:13:23.920 align:middle line:84%
If I look at what's in the
spectrum other than 0 possibly,

00:13:23.920 --> 00:13:27.310 align:middle line:84%
then the nonzero numbers
that are in the spectrum

00:13:27.310 --> 00:13:28.810 align:middle line:90%
have to be eigenvalues.

00:13:28.810 --> 00:13:34.120 align:middle line:90%


00:13:34.120 --> 00:13:37.050 align:middle line:84%
So for a compact
self-adjoint operator,

00:13:37.050 --> 00:13:42.780 align:middle line:84%
and just in the case of
matrices, the spectrum,

00:13:42.780 --> 00:13:45.390 align:middle line:90%
the nonzero spectrum has to be--

00:13:45.390 --> 00:13:47.790 align:middle line:90%
are nothing but eigenvalues.

00:13:47.790 --> 00:13:49.380 align:middle line:84%
And last time,
remember, we proved

00:13:49.380 --> 00:13:53.700 align:middle line:84%
that the eigenvalues
are countably infinite,

00:13:53.700 --> 00:13:54.750 align:middle line:90%
or countable.

00:13:54.750 --> 00:13:56.670 align:middle line:84%
They're either finite
or countably infinite.

00:13:56.670 --> 00:13:58.920 align:middle line:84%
And if they're countable
infinite, they converge to 0.

00:13:58.920 --> 00:14:04.560 align:middle line:90%


00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:12.460 align:middle line:84%
So, again, by last
lecture, we conclude

00:14:12.460 --> 00:14:20.040 align:middle line:84%
that the spectrum of
A take away 0 equals

00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:32.750 align:middle line:84%
either finitely many eigenvalues
or countably infinite

00:14:32.750 --> 00:14:38.790 align:middle line:84%
eigenvalues that
are converging to 0.

00:14:38.790 --> 00:14:44.690 align:middle line:90%


00:14:44.690 --> 00:14:48.373 align:middle line:84%
So from the Fredholm alternative
we get a lot of information

00:14:48.373 --> 00:14:49.790 align:middle line:84%
about when we can
solve equations.

00:14:49.790 --> 00:14:51.440 align:middle line:90%
But it also tells us--

00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:53.600 align:middle line:84%
I mean from that
ability to say when

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:57.650 align:middle line:84%
we can solve the
equations, we can also

00:14:57.650 --> 00:15:00.170 align:middle line:84%
characterize the
nonzero spectrum

00:15:00.170 --> 00:15:03.080 align:middle line:84%
of a self-adjoint
compact operator.

00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:11.940 align:middle line:90%


00:15:11.940 --> 00:15:13.878 align:middle line:90%
So we need to prove that--

00:15:13.878 --> 00:15:15.420 align:middle line:84%
so remember all of
this just followed

00:15:15.420 --> 00:15:20.125 align:middle line:84%
from stuff we had proven
and the main conclusion

00:15:20.125 --> 00:15:22.500 align:middle line:84%
of the theorem, which is that
the range of A minus lambda

00:15:22.500 --> 00:15:23.310 align:middle line:90%
is closed.

00:15:23.310 --> 00:15:26.250 align:middle line:84%
So we need to prove that
the range of A minus lambda

00:15:26.250 --> 00:15:29.080 align:middle line:84%
is closed when lambda is
a nonzero real number.

00:15:29.080 --> 00:15:36.210 align:middle line:84%
So suppose that you have a
sequence in the range, which

00:15:36.210 --> 00:15:39.900 align:middle line:84%
I'll write as A minus
lambda times un converging

00:15:39.900 --> 00:15:46.050 align:middle line:84%
to some element f in H. So what
we'd like to be able to show

00:15:46.050 --> 00:15:48.430 align:middle line:90%
is that f is in the range.

00:15:48.430 --> 00:16:01.930 align:middle line:84%
So we want to show f
is in the range of H--

00:16:01.930 --> 00:16:06.090 align:middle line:90%
or not range of H, range of A--

00:16:06.090 --> 00:16:08.020 align:middle line:90%
minus lambda, sorry.

00:16:08.020 --> 00:16:11.395 align:middle line:84%
Hopefully I didn't make that
mistake elsewhere-- no, just--

00:16:11.395 --> 00:16:11.895 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:16:11.895 --> 00:16:14.420 align:middle line:90%


00:16:14.420 --> 00:16:19.520 align:middle line:84%
Now, we're only assuming
that a minus lambda when

00:16:19.520 --> 00:16:21.230 align:middle line:90%
it hits u sub n converges to f.

00:16:21.230 --> 00:16:24.950 align:middle line:84%
We're not a priori assuming
the use of n's converge.

00:16:24.950 --> 00:16:25.730 align:middle line:90%
In fact, we can't.

00:16:25.730 --> 00:16:29.870 align:middle line:90%


00:16:29.870 --> 00:16:32.030 align:middle line:84%
But in the end we
would like to come up

00:16:32.030 --> 00:16:37.400 align:middle line:84%
with maybe a subsequence
or a part of the u sub n's

00:16:37.400 --> 00:16:39.410 align:middle line:84%
up to a subsequence
which does converge,

00:16:39.410 --> 00:16:42.380 align:middle line:84%
and then we conclude
that f is in the range.

00:16:42.380 --> 00:16:47.600 align:middle line:84%
So first I want to get rid of
the useless part of the u sub

00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:48.470 align:middle line:90%
n's.

00:16:48.470 --> 00:16:53.420 align:middle line:90%
So let W be--

00:16:53.420 --> 00:16:56.990 align:middle line:90%


00:16:56.990 --> 00:16:59.060 align:middle line:84%
well, I don't need
to give it a name.

00:16:59.060 --> 00:17:01.655 align:middle line:90%
Really, where is my eraser?

00:17:01.655 --> 00:17:11.119 align:middle line:90%


00:17:11.119 --> 00:17:19.819 align:middle line:84%
So let vn be the projection
onto orthogonal complement

00:17:19.819 --> 00:17:24.650 align:middle line:84%
of the null space of A
minus lambda of u sub n.

00:17:24.650 --> 00:17:30.440 align:middle line:90%


00:17:30.440 --> 00:17:38.150 align:middle line:90%
So now this is just part of--

00:17:38.150 --> 00:17:40.010 align:middle line:90%
so every u sub n is written--

00:17:40.010 --> 00:17:44.960 align:middle line:84%
you can write as something
in the null space of A minus

00:17:44.960 --> 00:17:47.660 align:middle line:90%
lambda.

00:17:47.660 --> 00:17:49.610 align:middle line:84%
So since null space
of A minus lambda

00:17:49.610 --> 00:17:54.680 align:middle line:84%
is a closed subspace of
H, it has an orthogonal

00:17:54.680 --> 00:17:58.160 align:middle line:84%
complement so that it and
its orthogonal complement

00:17:58.160 --> 00:18:01.730 align:middle line:90%
gives the direct product--

00:18:01.730 --> 00:18:04.280 align:middle line:84%
or when you take their
direct product, gives you H.

00:18:04.280 --> 00:18:09.060 align:middle line:90%
So why am I saying that?

00:18:09.060 --> 00:18:14.330 align:middle line:84%
Because then if I take
A minus lambda u sub n,

00:18:14.330 --> 00:18:18.200 align:middle line:84%
this is equal to A
minus lambda applied

00:18:18.200 --> 00:18:26.150 align:middle line:84%
to pi, so the projection
onto the null space of u sub

00:18:26.150 --> 00:18:29.300 align:middle line:84%
n plus the projection onto
the orthogonal complement

00:18:29.300 --> 00:18:33.200 align:middle line:84%
of the null space, which
I defined as v sub n.

00:18:33.200 --> 00:18:37.410 align:middle line:84%
Now, this element here is in the
null space of A minus lambda.

00:18:37.410 --> 00:18:39.500 align:middle line:84%
So when A minus lambda
hits it, I get 0.

00:18:39.500 --> 00:18:44.780 align:middle line:84%
So I get A minus lambda
applied to v sub n, all right.

00:18:44.780 --> 00:18:49.860 align:middle line:90%


00:18:49.860 --> 00:18:56.750 align:middle line:84%
Then a minus lambda v sub n
equals A minus lambda u sub

00:18:56.750 --> 00:18:58.310 align:middle line:90%
n, which converges to f.

00:18:58.310 --> 00:19:04.010 align:middle line:84%
So, basically, I've taken away
some noise, all right, the part

00:19:04.010 --> 00:19:06.650 align:middle line:84%
that when it hits A
minus lambda, I get 0.

00:19:06.650 --> 00:19:09.260 align:middle line:84%
So now I just have
these v sub n's

00:19:09.260 --> 00:19:22.080 align:middle line:84%
which lie in the orthogonal
complement of the null space

00:19:22.080 --> 00:19:25.250 align:middle line:90%
of A minus lambda.

00:19:25.250 --> 00:19:34.390 align:middle line:84%
So my claim is, first, is
that the sequence v sub

00:19:34.390 --> 00:19:41.350 align:middle line:90%
n is bounded.

00:19:41.350 --> 00:19:43.870 align:middle line:84%
Basically, once I can
show this, then I'm

00:19:43.870 --> 00:19:46.960 align:middle line:84%
done because if I can show
v sub n is bounded, then

00:19:46.960 --> 00:19:51.790 align:middle line:84%
since A is a compact operator,
when A hits v sub n up

00:19:51.790 --> 00:19:54.530 align:middle line:84%
to a subsequence,
that converges.

00:19:54.530 --> 00:19:56.680 align:middle line:84%
Now, this whole expression
converges and therefore

00:19:56.680 --> 00:19:58.900 align:middle line:90%
lambda times v sub n converges.

00:19:58.900 --> 00:20:01.900 align:middle line:84%
Lambda is nonzero, so
then v sub n converges up

00:20:01.900 --> 00:20:03.490 align:middle line:90%
to a subsequence to something.

00:20:03.490 --> 00:20:05.290 align:middle line:84%
And therefore A
minus lambda v sub n

00:20:05.290 --> 00:20:08.320 align:middle line:84%
then converges to
A minus lambda v

00:20:08.320 --> 00:20:11.410 align:middle line:84%
for some v, which
shows that f is

00:20:11.410 --> 00:20:13.760 align:middle line:90%
in the range of A minus lambda.

00:20:13.760 --> 00:20:17.290 align:middle line:84%
So this is really
the whole ball game.

00:20:17.290 --> 00:20:19.150 align:middle line:84%
And we'll use here
crucially as well

00:20:19.150 --> 00:20:21.820 align:middle line:84%
that we threw away
useless parts of u sub n,

00:20:21.820 --> 00:20:23.905 align:middle line:84%
useless at least
to this argument.

00:20:23.905 --> 00:20:27.030 align:middle line:90%


00:20:27.030 --> 00:20:31.330 align:middle line:84%
So I claim this is
bounded, so suppose not.

00:20:31.330 --> 00:20:36.000 align:middle line:90%


00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:43.970 align:middle line:84%
Then there exists
subsequence v sub n sub

00:20:43.970 --> 00:20:58.100 align:middle line:84%
j such that v sub n
sub j goes to infinity

00:20:58.100 --> 00:20:59.540 align:middle line:90%
as j goes to infinity.

00:20:59.540 --> 00:21:04.430 align:middle line:90%


00:21:04.430 --> 00:21:11.290 align:middle line:84%
All right, now if I look
at A minus lambda applied

00:21:11.290 --> 00:21:19.100 align:middle line:84%
to v sub n sub j over norm of
v sub n sub j, this converges--

00:21:19.100 --> 00:21:25.420 align:middle line:84%
so first off, since is a linear
operator, this is equal to 1

00:21:25.420 --> 00:21:30.730 align:middle line:84%
over norm v n sub j times
A minus lambda applied

00:21:30.730 --> 00:21:33.100 align:middle line:90%
to v sub n sub j.

00:21:33.100 --> 00:21:39.580 align:middle line:84%
And so this scalar, 1 over
norm v n sub j converges to 0.

00:21:39.580 --> 00:21:41.710 align:middle line:90%
This converges to f.

00:21:41.710 --> 00:21:45.400 align:middle line:90%
So I get the 0 vector in H.

00:21:45.400 --> 00:21:57.330 align:middle line:84%
So this thing converges to 0
in the Hilbert space H. Now,

00:21:57.330 --> 00:21:58.200 align:middle line:90%
why is that bad?

00:21:58.200 --> 00:22:02.740 align:middle line:84%
Because essentially what
this is going to say

00:22:02.740 --> 00:22:05.530 align:middle line:84%
is that there
exists some element,

00:22:05.530 --> 00:22:08.530 align:middle line:84%
or that this sequence converges
at least up to a subsequence

00:22:08.530 --> 00:22:11.410 align:middle line:84%
to an element v with
norm 1 because they

00:22:11.410 --> 00:22:14.770 align:middle line:84%
all have norm 1 so that A
minus lambda v equals 0.

00:22:14.770 --> 00:22:18.790 align:middle line:84%
But all of these are in the
null space of A minus lambda

00:22:18.790 --> 00:22:20.080 align:middle line:90%
and we get a contradiction.

00:22:20.080 --> 00:22:24.220 align:middle line:90%


00:22:24.220 --> 00:22:29.930 align:middle line:90%
So we have that A minus lambda--

00:22:29.930 --> 00:22:30.805 align:middle line:90%
so we have that part.

00:22:30.805 --> 00:22:38.940 align:middle line:90%


00:22:38.940 --> 00:22:48.000 align:middle line:84%
Since is A is a
compact operator,

00:22:48.000 --> 00:22:51.000 align:middle line:84%
there exists a subsequence,
so a further subsequence--

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:56.742 align:middle line:84%
I'm just going to call it n sub
k instead of n sub j sub k--

00:22:56.742 --> 00:23:07.430 align:middle line:84%
v sub n sub k of
v sub n sub j such

00:23:07.430 --> 00:23:14.860 align:middle line:84%
that the sequence a sub
v sub n sub k converges.

00:23:14.860 --> 00:23:18.880 align:middle line:90%


00:23:18.880 --> 00:23:23.080 align:middle line:84%
But then I get that
v sub n sub k--

00:23:23.080 --> 00:23:38.520 align:middle line:84%
or I should say v sub n sub
k over norm of v sub n sub k.

00:23:38.520 --> 00:23:40.620 align:middle line:90%
So these all have norm 1.

00:23:40.620 --> 00:23:46.530 align:middle line:84%
And A applied to something
that has unit length--

00:23:46.530 --> 00:23:51.690 align:middle line:84%
or the image by A of the closed
unit ball is a precompact--

00:23:51.690 --> 00:23:53.790 align:middle line:90%
or the closure of it is compact.

00:23:53.790 --> 00:23:59.250 align:middle line:84%
And therefore every sequence
has a convergent subsequence.

00:23:59.250 --> 00:24:05.190 align:middle line:84%
Then v sub n sub k over
norm of v sub n sub K, ,

00:24:05.190 --> 00:24:13.830 align:middle line:84%
this is equal to 1 over lambda
times A applied to v sub n sub

00:24:13.830 --> 00:24:22.370 align:middle line:84%
k over norm of v sub n sub k
minus A minus lambda applied

00:24:22.370 --> 00:24:23.820 align:middle line:90%
to v sub n sub k.

00:24:23.820 --> 00:24:28.620 align:middle line:90%


00:24:28.620 --> 00:24:32.880 align:middle line:84%
Now this sequence of
elements converges to 0.

00:24:32.880 --> 00:24:35.430 align:middle line:84%
That's, in fact,
what we just proved.

00:24:35.430 --> 00:24:40.440 align:middle line:84%
And this converges by how
we've taken this subsequence

00:24:40.440 --> 00:24:42.130 align:middle line:90%
because A is a compact operator.

00:24:42.130 --> 00:24:46.290 align:middle line:84%
So I have this sequence
of vectors is equal to--

00:24:46.290 --> 00:24:50.580 align:middle line:84%
and here we can divide by lambda
because lambda is nonzero.

00:24:50.580 --> 00:24:55.350 align:middle line:84%
It's equal to a linear
combination of two sequences

00:24:55.350 --> 00:24:58.480 align:middle line:90%
which converge.

00:24:58.480 --> 00:25:00.720 align:middle line:84%
And therefore we
get that v sub n

00:25:00.720 --> 00:25:16.320 align:middle line:84%
sub k over norm of v sub n sub
k, k converges to an element v.

00:25:16.320 --> 00:25:21.120 align:middle line:84%
And now the null space or
the orthogonal complement

00:25:21.120 --> 00:25:26.430 align:middle line:84%
of the null space of A minus
lambda, each of these is in--

00:25:26.430 --> 00:25:31.550 align:middle line:84%
remember the orthogonal
complement of the null space.

00:25:31.550 --> 00:25:35.950 align:middle line:84%
And since it's converging to
an element and this is closed,

00:25:35.950 --> 00:25:39.330 align:middle line:84%
this element has to
be in the same set.

00:25:39.330 --> 00:25:46.600 align:middle line:90%


00:25:46.600 --> 00:25:49.660 align:middle line:90%
So, again, this follows from--

00:25:49.660 --> 00:25:53.680 align:middle line:84%
the fact that v is in here is
because this set is closed.

00:25:53.680 --> 00:25:57.305 align:middle line:84%
The orthogonal complement of
any subset of a Hilbert space

00:25:57.305 --> 00:25:57.805 align:middle line:90%
is closed.

00:25:57.805 --> 00:26:09.310 align:middle line:90%


00:26:09.310 --> 00:26:13.270 align:middle line:84%
Then by continuity of the
norm, basically, the norm of v

00:26:13.270 --> 00:26:16.480 align:middle line:84%
has to be equal
to limit as k goes

00:26:16.480 --> 00:26:20.410 align:middle line:84%
to infinity of the
norm of the elements

00:26:20.410 --> 00:26:24.355 align:middle line:84%
converging to it,
which all equal 1.

00:26:24.355 --> 00:26:29.860 align:middle line:90%


00:26:29.860 --> 00:26:37.120 align:middle line:84%
And if I compute A minus
lambda applied to v,

00:26:37.120 --> 00:26:38.350 align:middle line:90%
this is equal to--

00:26:38.350 --> 00:26:42.040 align:middle line:84%
since the v sub n sub
k's over norm v sub n

00:26:42.040 --> 00:26:50.200 align:middle line:84%
sub k's are converging to v A
minus lambda applied to v sub n

00:26:50.200 --> 00:26:54.490 align:middle line:90%
sub k over norm v sub n sub k.

00:26:54.490 --> 00:26:59.410 align:middle line:84%
And, remember, this is a
subsequence of v sub n sub j's.

00:26:59.410 --> 00:27:01.660 align:middle line:84%
And when A minus
lambda hits that,

00:27:01.660 --> 00:27:03.460 align:middle line:90%
they're converging to 0, so--

00:27:03.460 --> 00:27:07.790 align:middle line:90%


00:27:07.790 --> 00:27:10.610 align:middle line:84%
all of that predicated
upon the assumption

00:27:10.610 --> 00:27:15.200 align:middle line:84%
that the norm of v sub n sub
k's converges to infinity

00:27:15.200 --> 00:27:21.080 align:middle line:84%
or that the sequence
is unbounded.

00:27:21.080 --> 00:27:27.680 align:middle line:84%
So we have this element in
the null space that has--

00:27:27.680 --> 00:27:30.620 align:middle line:84%
or the orthogonal
complement of the null space

00:27:30.620 --> 00:27:36.170 align:middle line:84%
that has norm 1 but
also gives you 0.

00:27:36.170 --> 00:27:46.500 align:middle line:84%
And therefore we get that v is
in the null space of A minus

00:27:46.500 --> 00:27:55.700 align:middle line:84%
lambda from this computation
and it's orthogonal complement.

00:27:55.700 --> 00:27:59.560 align:middle line:90%


00:27:59.560 --> 00:28:02.170 align:middle line:84%
But the only possible
vector that's

00:28:02.170 --> 00:28:06.460 align:middle line:84%
in a space and it's orthogonal
complement is the 0 vector,

00:28:06.460 --> 00:28:12.850 align:middle line:84%
or the only vector in a subspace
and its orthogonal complement

00:28:12.850 --> 00:28:16.060 align:middle line:90%
is the zero vector.

00:28:16.060 --> 00:28:21.420 align:middle line:84%
And therefore v
equals 0, which this

00:28:21.420 --> 00:28:24.450 align:middle line:84%
is a contradiction to the fact
that the norm of v equals 1.

00:28:24.450 --> 00:28:27.700 align:middle line:90%


00:28:27.700 --> 00:28:31.570 align:middle line:84%
So we started off with
the sequence v sub n's.

00:28:31.570 --> 00:28:33.640 align:middle line:84%
Assuming that they
are unbounded,

00:28:33.640 --> 00:28:41.080 align:middle line:84%
v sub n over norm v sub n is a
sequence of essentially-- yeah.

00:28:41.080 --> 00:28:43.480 align:middle line:84%
So maybe you got lost
in the subsequences.

00:28:43.480 --> 00:28:45.280 align:middle line:84%
But let's just
assume I'm talking

00:28:45.280 --> 00:28:48.610 align:middle line:90%
about the entire sequence.

00:28:48.610 --> 00:28:52.210 align:middle line:84%
Then v sub n over
norm v sub n, when a.

00:28:52.210 --> 00:28:55.090 align:middle line:84%
Minus lambda hits
it, converges to 0.

00:28:55.090 --> 00:28:56.770 align:middle line:84%
Since A is a
compact operator, we

00:28:56.770 --> 00:29:02.290 align:middle line:84%
can show essentially that A
applied to v sub n over norm v

00:29:02.290 --> 00:29:04.840 align:middle line:84%
sub n, because those
things have unit length,

00:29:04.840 --> 00:29:06.430 align:middle line:90%
converges to something.

00:29:06.430 --> 00:29:08.740 align:middle line:84%
And since lambda is
nonzero, we can then

00:29:08.740 --> 00:29:14.330 align:middle line:84%
conclude that those
vectors converge,

00:29:14.330 --> 00:29:16.400 align:middle line:84%
in fact, to something,
not just their images

00:29:16.400 --> 00:29:19.740 align:middle line:84%
by A or their images
by A minus lambda,

00:29:19.740 --> 00:29:22.940 align:middle line:84%
again, because
lambda is nonzero.

00:29:22.940 --> 00:29:25.250 align:middle line:84%
And since they all
have unit length,

00:29:25.250 --> 00:29:28.020 align:middle line:84%
their limit must
have unit length.

00:29:28.020 --> 00:29:33.450 align:middle line:84%
And since when a minus lambda
hits these guys they go to 0,

00:29:33.450 --> 00:29:37.830 align:middle line:84%
the limit must also, when A
minus lambda hit it, equals 0.

00:29:37.830 --> 00:29:39.330 align:middle line:84%
And that gives us
our contradiction

00:29:39.330 --> 00:29:42.930 align:middle line:84%
because this limit v had to be
in the orthogonal complement

00:29:42.930 --> 00:29:45.690 align:middle line:84%
of the null space, but then
also in the null space,

00:29:45.690 --> 00:29:47.910 align:middle line:90%
and have unit length.

00:29:47.910 --> 00:29:50.370 align:middle line:84%
Those three things can
all happen at once.

00:29:50.370 --> 00:29:53.010 align:middle line:90%


00:29:53.010 --> 00:30:05.630 align:middle line:84%
So thus the sequence
v sub n is bounded.

00:30:05.630 --> 00:30:09.940 align:middle line:84%
So, remember, what were the
v sub n's to start with?

00:30:09.940 --> 00:30:12.115 align:middle line:84%
They were so that
A minus lambda v

00:30:12.115 --> 00:30:14.500 align:middle line:84%
sub n's converged
to this element f.

00:30:14.500 --> 00:30:17.950 align:middle line:84%
And we wanted to show
that f is in the range.

00:30:17.950 --> 00:30:21.940 align:middle line:90%
So we coming back over here.

00:30:21.940 --> 00:30:24.220 align:middle line:84%
We had these v sub n's
so that A minus lambda

00:30:24.220 --> 00:30:26.038 align:middle line:90%
v sub n converges to f.

00:30:26.038 --> 00:30:27.580 align:middle line:84%
And we want to show
f is in the range

00:30:27.580 --> 00:30:32.870 align:middle line:84%
to conclude that the range
of A minus lambda is closed.

00:30:32.870 --> 00:30:34.760 align:middle line:84%
But now since it's
bounded and we've

00:30:34.760 --> 00:30:39.440 align:middle line:84%
done this argument already, this
is essentially the whole ball

00:30:39.440 --> 00:30:39.940 align:middle line:90%
game.

00:30:39.940 --> 00:30:42.970 align:middle line:90%


00:30:42.970 --> 00:30:47.420 align:middle line:84%
Since the subsequence
v sub n is bounded,

00:30:47.420 --> 00:30:59.180 align:middle line:84%
and A is a compact operator ,
we conclude that there exists

00:30:59.180 --> 00:31:06.150 align:middle line:84%
a subsequence v sub
n sub j such that--

00:31:06.150 --> 00:31:08.640 align:middle line:84%
so this has nothing to do with
the previous argument now,

00:31:08.640 --> 00:31:12.060 align:middle line:84%
but I just don't feel like
using different letters--

00:31:12.060 --> 00:31:20.230 align:middle line:84%
such that a applied to
v sub n sub j converges.

00:31:20.230 --> 00:31:22.280 align:middle line:84%
So, remember, is A
compact operator,

00:31:22.280 --> 00:31:27.370 align:middle line:84%
which we stated it in
terms of the closure

00:31:27.370 --> 00:31:30.640 align:middle line:84%
of the image of the closed
unit ball being compact.

00:31:30.640 --> 00:31:35.850 align:middle line:84%
Equivalently by
scaling the unit ball,

00:31:35.850 --> 00:31:39.060 align:middle line:84%
it means that A takes
any bounded sequence

00:31:39.060 --> 00:31:43.350 align:middle line:84%
to a sequence that has a
convergent subsequence.

00:31:43.350 --> 00:31:45.910 align:middle line:90%


00:31:45.910 --> 00:31:47.650 align:middle line:90%
So we showed v sub n is bounded.

00:31:47.650 --> 00:31:49.900 align:middle line:84%
And therefore since A
is a compact operator,

00:31:49.900 --> 00:31:52.750 align:middle line:84%
we can find a subsequence
so that when A hits it,

00:31:52.750 --> 00:31:56.120 align:middle line:90%
we have a convergence sequence.

00:31:56.120 --> 00:31:58.160 align:middle line:84%
And by that same trick
we used a minute ago,

00:31:58.160 --> 00:32:01.010 align:middle line:84%
we conclude that v sub
n sub j, which is--

00:32:01.010 --> 00:32:03.320 align:middle line:84%
and we can do this
because lambda is nonzero.

00:32:03.320 --> 00:32:07.120 align:middle line:90%
We can divide by it.

00:32:07.120 --> 00:32:15.560 align:middle line:84%
So A v sub n sub j minus A
minus lambda V sub n sub j--

00:32:15.560 --> 00:32:20.000 align:middle line:84%
now, again, this here is
converging to something.

00:32:20.000 --> 00:32:22.100 align:middle line:90%
This here is converging to f.

00:32:22.100 --> 00:32:26.870 align:middle line:84%
So this linear combination
of convergent sequences

00:32:26.870 --> 00:32:27.920 align:middle line:90%
is convergent--

00:32:27.920 --> 00:32:32.680 align:middle line:90%


00:32:32.680 --> 00:32:43.200 align:middle line:90%
converges to and element v.

00:32:43.200 --> 00:32:49.170 align:middle line:84%
And therefore I get that f
which is the limit as n goes

00:32:49.170 --> 00:32:50.580 align:middle line:90%
to infinity of the v sub n's.

00:32:50.580 --> 00:32:54.960 align:middle line:84%
But convergence still holds
if I look at a subsequence,

00:32:54.960 --> 00:32:59.370 align:middle line:84%
A minus lambda v
sub n sub j equals--

00:32:59.370 --> 00:33:02.790 align:middle line:84%
and since A-- so A is a
bounded linear operator.

00:33:02.790 --> 00:33:05.280 align:middle line:84%
Lambda times the identity is
a bounded linear operator.

00:33:05.280 --> 00:33:09.810 align:middle line:84%
This is equal to A minus
lambda v. And therefore f is

00:33:09.810 --> 00:33:17.740 align:middle line:90%
in the range of A minus lambda.

00:33:17.740 --> 00:33:23.850 align:middle line:84%
OK, so Fredholm
alternative tells you

00:33:23.850 --> 00:33:28.820 align:middle line:84%
that the range of A minus lambda
for a compact self-adjoint

00:33:28.820 --> 00:33:29.825 align:middle line:90%
operator is closed.

00:33:29.825 --> 00:33:35.850 align:middle line:90%


00:33:35.850 --> 00:33:41.800 align:middle line:84%
So where did we really used the
fact that it was self adjoint?

00:33:41.800 --> 00:33:43.030 align:middle line:90%
Nowhere in this argument.

00:33:43.030 --> 00:33:49.030 align:middle line:84%
So this fact that the range
of A minus lambda is close

00:33:49.030 --> 00:33:54.460 align:middle line:84%
is still true if A is
just a compact operator

00:33:54.460 --> 00:33:57.955 align:middle line:84%
and lambda is just some
nonzero complex number.

00:33:57.955 --> 00:34:00.600 align:middle line:90%


00:34:00.600 --> 00:34:03.750 align:middle line:84%
But where we use that
it's self adjoint is I

00:34:03.750 --> 00:34:05.400 align:middle line:84%
guess in the rest
of the conclusion,

00:34:05.400 --> 00:34:08.400 align:middle line:84%
that the range of A
minus lambda is therefore

00:34:08.400 --> 00:34:10.350 align:middle line:84%
equal to the null space
of A minus lambda.

00:34:10.350 --> 00:34:13.920 align:middle line:84%
And therefore either A
minus lambda is bijective,

00:34:13.920 --> 00:34:16.440 align:middle line:84%
or the null space
of A minus lambda

00:34:16.440 --> 00:34:19.590 align:middle line:84%
is non-trivial and finite
dimensional by what

00:34:19.590 --> 00:34:21.180 align:middle line:90%
we did in the previous lecture.

00:34:21.180 --> 00:34:31.409 align:middle line:90%


00:34:31.409 --> 00:34:38.974 align:middle line:84%
OK, now, again, this is
a very powerful theorem.

00:34:38.974 --> 00:34:49.550 align:middle line:90%


00:34:49.550 --> 00:34:51.889 align:middle line:84%
And, again, what this
says is that if I

00:34:51.889 --> 00:34:54.620 align:middle line:84%
look at the nonzero spectrum
of a compact self-adjoint

00:34:54.620 --> 00:34:58.880 align:middle line:84%
operator, then that consists
entirely of eigenvalues of A.

00:34:58.880 --> 00:35:02.900 align:middle line:84%
Now earlier we proved that
plus or minus the norm of A

00:35:02.900 --> 00:35:08.090 align:middle line:84%
has to be in the spectrum
of a self-adjoint operator.

00:35:08.090 --> 00:35:10.250 align:middle line:84%
And therefore what
we can conclude

00:35:10.250 --> 00:35:14.330 align:middle line:84%
is that if we have a non-trivial
self-adjoint compact operator,

00:35:14.330 --> 00:35:18.860 align:middle line:84%
then it has at least
one eigenvalue.

00:35:18.860 --> 00:35:21.030 align:middle line:84%
And we can characterize
that eigenvalue.

00:35:21.030 --> 00:35:23.760 align:middle line:90%
So this has a following theorem.

00:35:23.760 --> 00:35:40.640 align:middle line:84%
Let A be a non-trivial
compact self-adjoint operator,

00:35:40.640 --> 00:35:41.495 align:middle line:90%
A equals A star.

00:35:41.495 --> 00:35:50.140 align:middle line:90%


00:35:50.140 --> 00:36:10.660 align:middle line:84%
Then has a non-trivial
eigenvalue lambda 1.

00:36:10.660 --> 00:36:14.290 align:middle line:84%
And we can
characterize lambda 1--

00:36:14.290 --> 00:36:16.540 align:middle line:84%
or at least the absolute
value of lambda 1--

00:36:16.540 --> 00:36:33.900 align:middle line:84%
as the supremum over norm
u equals 1 Au, u equals--

00:36:33.900 --> 00:36:43.130 align:middle line:84%
and this supremum
is actually achieved

00:36:43.130 --> 00:36:55.210 align:middle line:84%
where u1 is a
normalized eigenvector

00:36:55.210 --> 00:36:56.760 align:middle line:90%
corresponding to lambda 1.

00:36:56.760 --> 00:37:07.460 align:middle line:90%


00:37:07.460 --> 00:37:12.300 align:middle line:90%
So why do we have--

00:37:12.300 --> 00:37:16.890 align:middle line:84%
or I should say, let me make
sure I have everything here.

00:37:16.890 --> 00:37:18.885 align:middle line:90%
OK, so why is this?

00:37:18.885 --> 00:37:24.430 align:middle line:90%


00:37:24.430 --> 00:37:30.940 align:middle line:84%
So first off, we've shown that
plus or minus the norm of A is,

00:37:30.940 --> 00:37:40.310 align:middle line:84%
in fact, in the spectrum of A.
for any self-adjoint bounded

00:37:40.310 --> 00:37:48.040 align:middle line:84%
linear operator, not necessarily
compact, that plus or minus--

00:37:48.040 --> 00:37:51.010 align:middle line:84%
not necessarily both of
them, but plus or minus,

00:37:51.010 --> 00:38:00.760 align:middle line:84%
one of these, at least one
of plus or minus, since a

00:38:00.760 --> 00:38:15.360 align:middle line:84%
is self adjoint,
meaning A star equals A.

00:38:15.360 --> 00:38:22.460 align:middle line:84%
Then lambda 1-- then I'm
going to say plus or minus,

00:38:22.460 --> 00:38:29.980 align:middle line:84%
meaning not both, actually
at least one of these,

00:38:29.980 --> 00:38:34.850 align:middle line:84%
is an eigenvalue of A by
the Fredholm alternative.

00:38:34.850 --> 00:38:45.960 align:middle line:90%


00:38:45.960 --> 00:38:56.935 align:middle line:84%
The Fredholm alternative
and the fact that--

00:38:56.935 --> 00:39:00.350 align:middle line:90%


00:39:00.350 --> 00:39:06.500 align:middle line:84%
so lambda one is going
to be either plus norm

00:39:06.500 --> 00:39:10.040 align:middle line:84%
of A or minus norm
of A, depending on

00:39:10.040 --> 00:39:13.700 align:middle line:84%
whether which one
is in the spectrum.

00:39:13.700 --> 00:39:18.590 align:middle line:84%
Let's say plus if it's in the
spectrum, minus if plus is not.

00:39:18.590 --> 00:39:24.380 align:middle line:84%
And the fact that we can
identify it as this quantity

00:39:24.380 --> 00:39:32.480 align:middle line:84%
here is because we have that
for self-adjoint operators,

00:39:32.480 --> 00:39:37.790 align:middle line:84%
norm of A, which is the absolute
value of one of those plus

00:39:37.790 --> 00:39:41.030 align:middle line:84%
or minuses that are in
the spectrum is equal to--

00:39:41.030 --> 00:39:42.680 align:middle line:90%
so this is where--

00:39:42.680 --> 00:39:53.370 align:middle line:84%
so this is equal to sup
u equals 1, Au equals u.

00:39:53.370 --> 00:40:01.290 align:middle line:90%


00:40:01.290 --> 00:40:05.200 align:middle line:90%
So that's the end of the proof.

00:40:05.200 --> 00:40:10.800 align:middle line:84%
And now what we're going to do
is we are going to keep going.

00:40:10.800 --> 00:40:13.920 align:middle line:84%
So the end result
will be that we

00:40:13.920 --> 00:40:19.700 align:middle line:84%
can determine all
of the eigenvalues

00:40:19.700 --> 00:40:24.950 align:middle line:84%
via a certain maximum
principle and build up

00:40:24.950 --> 00:40:28.010 align:middle line:84%
a sequence of eigenvectors,
normalized eigenvectors, which

00:40:28.010 --> 00:40:31.100 align:middle line:84%
are pairwise orthogonal
simply because they

00:40:31.100 --> 00:40:35.500 align:middle line:90%
come from how they're built up.

00:40:35.500 --> 00:40:38.270 align:middle line:90%
But we'll see.

00:40:38.270 --> 00:40:40.520 align:middle line:90%
And what?

00:40:40.520 --> 00:40:43.670 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll show
that essentially that

00:40:43.670 --> 00:40:48.260 align:middle line:84%
set of eigenvectors that we
get, along with a set of--

00:40:48.260 --> 00:40:52.190 align:middle line:84%
or an orthonormal basis chosen
for the null space of A,

00:40:52.190 --> 00:40:57.890 align:middle line:84%
form an orthonormal basis for
a separable Hilbert space H.

00:40:57.890 --> 00:40:59.820 align:middle line:90%
So that's where we're going.

00:40:59.820 --> 00:41:05.550 align:middle line:84%
But we can basically take this
theorem and keep applying it.

00:41:05.550 --> 00:41:08.210 align:middle line:84%
So we have the following
maximum principle.

00:41:08.210 --> 00:41:11.300 align:middle line:90%


00:41:11.300 --> 00:41:13.940 align:middle line:84%
If you like, this is the first
step in a maximum principle.

00:41:13.940 --> 00:41:16.545 align:middle line:84%
This says if you want to find
the largest eigenvalue, lambda

00:41:16.545 --> 00:41:17.045 align:middle line:90%
1--

00:41:17.045 --> 00:41:20.880 align:middle line:90%


00:41:20.880 --> 00:41:22.200 align:middle line:90%
I should say--

00:41:22.200 --> 00:41:29.170 align:middle line:84%
I can even say largest
eigenvalue, largest

00:41:29.170 --> 00:41:34.620 align:middle line:90%
in the sense of absolute value.

00:41:34.620 --> 00:41:35.120 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:41:35.120 --> 00:41:37.370 align:middle line:84%
Because remember the
spectrum is contained

00:41:37.370 --> 00:41:43.250 align:middle line:84%
in the interval minus
norm A plus norm of A.

00:41:43.250 --> 00:41:48.290 align:middle line:84%
So anything in the spectrum
has to have absolute value less

00:41:48.290 --> 00:41:53.950 align:middle line:84%
than or equal to the norm of
A. And if it's eigenvalue,

00:41:53.950 --> 00:41:56.690 align:middle line:90%
or if it's a--

00:41:56.690 --> 00:42:02.940 align:middle line:84%
anything other than 0 has to be
an eigenvalue so we get this.

00:42:02.940 --> 00:42:05.740 align:middle line:84%
So what's this
maximum principle?

00:42:05.740 --> 00:42:06.990 align:middle line:90%
And why was I saying all that?

00:42:06.990 --> 00:42:09.240 align:middle line:90%
Oh, so this gives you a way to--

00:42:09.240 --> 00:42:11.760 align:middle line:84%
if you'd like try
and find or at least

00:42:11.760 --> 00:42:18.000 align:middle line:84%
approximate the first eigenvalue
of a bounded linear operator,

00:42:18.000 --> 00:42:21.930 align:middle line:84%
this is a maximization
problem with a constraint.

00:42:21.930 --> 00:42:24.510 align:middle line:84%
So you could use the method
of Lagrange multipliers.

00:42:24.510 --> 00:42:27.090 align:middle line:90%


00:42:27.090 --> 00:42:30.920 align:middle line:84%
That doesn't maybe
make sense to you

00:42:30.920 --> 00:42:33.410 align:middle line:84%
to be able to do on an infinite
dimensional Hilbert space.

00:42:33.410 --> 00:42:38.270 align:middle line:84%
But let's say you choose a big
basis of your Hilbert space.

00:42:38.270 --> 00:42:42.530 align:middle line:84%
And you just restrict to
looking at that big but finite

00:42:42.530 --> 00:42:49.170 align:middle line:84%
dimensional, or finite basis,
or span of that finite basis,

00:42:49.170 --> 00:42:51.460 align:middle line:84%
and try to solve the
approximate problem,

00:42:51.460 --> 00:42:56.990 align:middle line:84%
then you should get
close to the eigenvalue

00:42:56.990 --> 00:42:59.390 align:middle line:84%
and get an approximate
eigenvector

00:42:59.390 --> 00:43:04.690 align:middle line:84%
because as I've said, the
eigenvector will be achieved--

00:43:04.690 --> 00:43:07.910 align:middle line:84%
or I should say that the
eigenvector achieves this

00:43:07.910 --> 00:43:10.990 align:middle line:90%
maximum here , or the supremum.

00:43:10.990 --> 00:43:14.780 align:middle line:84%
So the maximum principle
is the following.

00:43:14.780 --> 00:43:20.320 align:middle line:84%
So let A-- again, we're only
looking at compact self-adjoint

00:43:20.320 --> 00:43:30.790 align:middle line:90%
operators, compact operator--

00:43:30.790 --> 00:43:35.520 align:middle line:90%


00:43:35.520 --> 00:43:50.320 align:middle line:84%
then the nonzero eigenvalues
of A can be ordered.

00:43:50.320 --> 00:43:52.780 align:middle line:90%
Now, this part we already know.

00:43:52.780 --> 00:43:57.340 align:middle line:84%
They can be ordered lambda 1
less than or equal to lambda 2,

00:43:57.340 --> 00:44:02.410 align:middle line:84%
less than or equal to lambda
3, counted with multiplicity

00:44:02.410 --> 00:44:08.010 align:middle line:84%
meaning if lambda 1 has a
two-dimensional eigenspace then

00:44:08.010 --> 00:44:08.910 align:middle line:90%
lambda 2--

00:44:08.910 --> 00:44:11.040 align:middle line:90%
or the absolute value of--

00:44:11.040 --> 00:44:13.050 align:middle line:90%
or lambda 2 will be lambda 1.

00:44:13.050 --> 00:44:16.620 align:middle line:84%
So we'll repeat it
according to multiplicity.

00:44:16.620 --> 00:44:32.840 align:middle line:90%


00:44:32.840 --> 00:44:35.600 align:middle line:84%
So we know that there's finitely
many distinct eigenvalues.

00:44:35.600 --> 00:44:37.100 align:middle line:84%
That was the part
that I was saying.

00:44:37.100 --> 00:44:43.780 align:middle line:84%
We know we can order them,
but maybe it's not clear

00:44:43.780 --> 00:44:53.850 align:middle line:84%
that you can order
them with multiplicity

00:44:53.850 --> 00:45:15.210 align:middle line:84%
with corresponding orthonormal
eigenbasis functions uk.

00:45:15.210 --> 00:45:22.500 align:middle line:84%
So u1 is a normalized
eigenfunction for lambda 1.

00:45:22.500 --> 00:45:25.710 align:middle line:84%
u2 will be a normalized
function for lambda 2, which

00:45:25.710 --> 00:45:27.570 align:middle line:90%
is orthogonal to u1.

00:45:27.570 --> 00:45:30.090 align:middle line:84%
So these are
pairwise orthonormal.

00:45:30.090 --> 00:45:32.750 align:middle line:90%


00:45:32.750 --> 00:45:38.340 align:middle line:84%
And how do we obtain
the eigenvalues

00:45:38.340 --> 00:45:40.140 align:middle line:84%
in this order and
these eigenfunctions

00:45:40.140 --> 00:45:48.860 align:middle line:84%
via the following
process so that lambda j

00:45:48.860 --> 00:45:58.770 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the supremum
over all unit vectors that

00:45:58.770 --> 00:46:03.090 align:middle line:84%
are orthogonal to
the first j minus 1?

00:46:03.090 --> 00:46:19.610 align:middle line:90%


00:46:19.610 --> 00:46:21.320 align:middle line:90%
And this is equal to--

00:46:21.320 --> 00:46:24.180 align:middle line:84%
this will be achieved
on the u sub j.

00:46:24.180 --> 00:46:29.950 align:middle line:90%


00:46:29.950 --> 00:46:35.480 align:middle line:84%
So we have the first
one, if you like.

00:46:35.480 --> 00:46:36.810 align:middle line:90%
We built up lambda 1, u1.

00:46:36.810 --> 00:46:37.310 align:middle line:90%
and.

00:46:37.310 --> 00:46:39.740 align:middle line:84%
Now what this maximum
principle theorem

00:46:39.740 --> 00:46:42.290 align:middle line:84%
is saying is that
we can repeat this,

00:46:42.290 --> 00:46:46.280 align:middle line:84%
is that if we now look
at this quantity here,

00:46:46.280 --> 00:46:50.210 align:middle line:84%
the supremum over
all norm 1 vectors

00:46:50.210 --> 00:46:55.250 align:middle line:84%
that are orthogonal
to u1, then we're

00:46:55.250 --> 00:47:00.920 align:middle line:84%
going to pick up the next
largest eigenvalue counted

00:47:00.920 --> 00:47:05.630 align:middle line:84%
with multiplicity, meaning it'll
be the absolute value of lambda

00:47:05.630 --> 00:47:07.700 align:middle line:84%
2, which will be
less than lambda 1

00:47:07.700 --> 00:47:15.200 align:middle line:84%
if lambda 1 only has a one
dimensional eigenspace.

00:47:15.200 --> 00:47:18.470 align:middle line:84%
Or it'll be lambda-- or the
absolute value of lambda 2

00:47:18.470 --> 00:47:20.270 align:middle line:84%
will be the absolute
value of lambda 1

00:47:20.270 --> 00:47:23.330 align:middle line:84%
again if lambda 1
has a, let's say,

00:47:23.330 --> 00:47:26.250 align:middle line:90%
two-dimensional eigenspace.

00:47:26.250 --> 00:47:28.140 align:middle line:84%
And if it had a
three-dimensional eigenspace,

00:47:28.140 --> 00:47:32.970 align:middle line:84%
we would get the absolute
value of lambda 1 for--

00:47:32.970 --> 00:47:36.160 align:middle line:84%
it would equal this number
and this number as well.

00:47:36.160 --> 00:47:42.750 align:middle line:84%
So, again, these
nonzero eigenvalues

00:47:42.750 --> 00:47:44.310 align:middle line:90%
can be ordered in this way.

00:47:44.310 --> 00:47:49.000 align:middle line:90%


00:47:49.000 --> 00:47:50.170 align:middle line:90%
Oh, and I left off one.

00:47:50.170 --> 00:47:54.720 align:middle line:90%


00:47:54.720 --> 00:47:56.235 align:middle line:90%
And the lambda j is going to 0.

00:47:56.235 --> 00:48:08.100 align:middle line:90%


00:48:08.100 --> 00:48:14.200 align:middle line:84%
OK, so we have the first one,
the absolute value of lambda 1,

00:48:14.200 --> 00:48:15.980 align:middle line:90%
and the first eigenvector u1.

00:48:15.980 --> 00:48:16.480 align:middle line:90%
and.

00:48:16.480 --> 00:48:22.680 align:middle line:84%
Now we're just going to
basically repeat or apply

00:48:22.680 --> 00:48:34.380 align:middle line:84%
the previous theorem to a
modification of A. Now I should

00:48:34.380 --> 00:48:41.160 align:middle line:84%
say this is not entirely
true because there may only

00:48:41.160 --> 00:48:42.105 align:middle line:90%
be finitely many.

00:48:42.105 --> 00:48:45.280 align:middle line:90%


00:48:45.280 --> 00:48:48.130 align:middle line:84%
So really this
should be a remark,

00:48:48.130 --> 00:49:14.010 align:middle line:84%
if the decreasing sequence
does not terminate,

00:49:14.010 --> 00:49:18.300 align:middle line:84%
then the absolute value of
the lambda j's goes to 0.

00:49:18.300 --> 00:49:31.270 align:middle line:90%


00:49:31.270 --> 00:49:42.790 align:middle line:84%
Now, so what's the new
bit of information here?

00:49:42.790 --> 00:49:45.810 align:middle line:90%
We do know that we can--

00:49:45.810 --> 00:49:50.280 align:middle line:84%
for each, let's say, capital
N, the number of eigenvalues

00:49:50.280 --> 00:49:51.840 align:middle line:90%
outside of--

00:49:51.840 --> 00:49:56.850 align:middle line:84%
or with absolute value bigger
than 1 over N has to be finite.

00:49:56.850 --> 00:49:59.550 align:middle line:84%
So the fact that
we can order them

00:49:59.550 --> 00:50:02.670 align:middle line:84%
is not really that
much new information.

00:50:02.670 --> 00:50:05.580 align:middle line:84%
And the fact that they to go to
0 if this sequence is infinite,

00:50:05.580 --> 00:50:07.470 align:middle line:90%
that's also not new information.

00:50:07.470 --> 00:50:10.110 align:middle line:84%
I mean, we did prove that
in a previous lecture

00:50:10.110 --> 00:50:10.890 align:middle line:90%
that if you have--

00:50:10.890 --> 00:50:15.450 align:middle line:90%


00:50:15.450 --> 00:50:17.940 align:middle line:84%
so there we proved
it for a sequence

00:50:17.940 --> 00:50:19.140 align:middle line:90%
of distinct eigenvalues.

00:50:19.140 --> 00:50:21.420 align:middle line:84%
But because we now
know that each of these

00:50:21.420 --> 00:50:25.350 align:middle line:84%
has a finite dimensional
eigenspace, if you just

00:50:25.350 --> 00:50:27.840 align:middle line:84%
look back at the same
proof or that proof,

00:50:27.840 --> 00:50:29.400 align:middle line:84%
you can make a small
adjustment to be

00:50:29.400 --> 00:50:33.690 align:middle line:84%
able to say that if you
count the eigenvalues

00:50:33.690 --> 00:50:37.290 align:middle line:84%
with multiplicity, then also
the sequence has to go to 0,

00:50:37.290 --> 00:50:39.270 align:middle line:84%
not just necessarily
the sequence consisting

00:50:39.270 --> 00:50:41.680 align:middle line:90%
of the distinct eigenvalues.

00:50:41.680 --> 00:50:48.120 align:middle line:84%
So let me make
that small remark.

00:50:48.120 --> 00:50:49.980 align:middle line:84%
What the new piece
is that we can

00:50:49.980 --> 00:50:52.710 align:middle line:84%
compute the
eigenvalues in this way

00:50:52.710 --> 00:50:57.300 align:middle line:84%
and choose the
eigenvectors in this way.

00:50:57.300 --> 00:50:57.800 align:middle line:90%
That's a.

00:50:57.800 --> 00:51:00.680 align:middle line:84%
New piece and that's
what, in the end,

00:51:00.680 --> 00:51:06.110 align:middle line:84%
we'll use to be able
to show that H can be--

00:51:06.110 --> 00:51:08.720 align:middle line:84%
or that A can basically
be diagonalized,

00:51:08.720 --> 00:51:12.380 align:middle line:84%
or that you can find an
orthonormal basis of a Hilbert

00:51:12.380 --> 00:51:15.170 align:middle line:84%
space, separable Hilbert
space consisting entirely

00:51:15.170 --> 00:51:19.880 align:middle line:90%
of eigenvectors of A.

00:51:19.880 --> 00:51:35.990 align:middle line:84%
So the construction
proceeds inductively.

00:51:35.990 --> 00:51:39.877 align:middle line:84%
So at one point I said,
if you can d it for one

00:51:39.877 --> 00:51:41.210 align:middle line:90%
then you can do it for the next.

00:51:41.210 --> 00:51:43.250 align:middle line:84%
And then I won't be
so formal every time

00:51:43.250 --> 00:51:46.640 align:middle line:90%
I do an inductive construction.

00:51:46.640 --> 00:51:52.270 align:middle line:84%
But in this case it
pays to be careful.

00:51:52.270 --> 00:51:57.760 align:middle line:84%
So we'll construct the
sequence of lambda j's

00:51:57.760 --> 00:52:06.830 align:middle line:84%
and uk's in this way via
an inductive argument.

00:52:06.830 --> 00:52:13.570 align:middle line:84%
So coming up with k equals 1,
that was the previous theorem.

00:52:13.570 --> 00:52:21.450 align:middle line:84%
So previous-- or I
should say j equals

00:52:21.450 --> 00:52:26.670 align:middle line:90%
1, that's the previous theorem.

00:52:26.670 --> 00:52:31.050 align:middle line:84%
We found the largest eigenvalue,
or the eigenvalue with--

00:52:31.050 --> 00:52:34.140 align:middle line:84%
an eigenvalue with the
largest absolute value

00:52:34.140 --> 00:52:36.720 align:middle line:90%
via this theorem.

00:52:36.720 --> 00:52:40.990 align:middle line:84%
And then we obtained an
eigenvector this way,

00:52:40.990 --> 00:52:44.380 align:middle line:84%
just basically as a consequence
of the Fredholm alternative.

00:52:44.380 --> 00:52:46.780 align:middle line:84%
And then that we know that
plus or minus the norm of A

00:52:46.780 --> 00:52:48.620 align:middle line:90%
has to be in the spectrum.

00:52:48.620 --> 00:52:52.750 align:middle line:84%
| the fact that we can find the
first eigenvalue of lambda 1,

00:52:52.750 --> 00:52:54.470 align:middle line:84%
that follows from
the previous theorem.

00:52:54.470 --> 00:53:04.130 align:middle line:84%
So now we want to do
the inductive step, i.e.

00:53:04.130 --> 00:53:19.640 align:middle line:84%
we want to suppose we have
found lambda 1 up to lambda--

00:53:19.640 --> 00:53:22.740 align:middle line:90%
let's say, what did I use here?

00:53:22.740 --> 00:53:41.420 align:middle line:84%
Lambda n, so j equals n and
with orthonormal eigenvectors

00:53:41.420 --> 00:53:51.370 align:middle line:84%
u1 up to un, satisfying
this maximum principal.

00:53:51.370 --> 00:53:56.410 align:middle line:84%
So j starting
from-- starting at 1

00:53:56.410 --> 00:54:01.270 align:middle line:84%
up to n, we've constructed or
found a lambda 2, lambda 3, up

00:54:01.270 --> 00:54:05.760 align:middle line:84%
to lambda n, and
the u went up to un,

00:54:05.760 --> 00:54:08.760 align:middle line:84%
satisfying that
maximum property.

00:54:08.760 --> 00:54:14.820 align:middle line:90%


00:54:14.820 --> 00:54:19.445 align:middle line:90%
So then there's two cases.

00:54:19.445 --> 00:54:23.900 align:middle line:90%


00:54:23.900 --> 00:54:29.870 align:middle line:84%
So in case 1 is the
fact that A minus--

00:54:29.870 --> 00:54:34.330 align:middle line:90%


00:54:34.330 --> 00:54:42.400 align:middle line:84%
A is equal to the sum from
k equals 1 to n of lambda

00:54:42.400 --> 00:54:46.675 align:middle line:90%
k, u inner product uk, uk.

00:54:46.675 --> 00:54:50.130 align:middle line:90%


00:54:50.130 --> 00:55:04.270 align:middle line:84%
And therefore what
this shows is that we

00:55:04.270 --> 00:55:11.870 align:middle line:84%
found all the eigenvalues
and the process terminates.

00:55:11.870 --> 00:55:19.297 align:middle line:90%


00:55:19.297 --> 00:55:20.880 align:middle line:84%
So this is kind of
the degenerate case

00:55:20.880 --> 00:55:23.913 align:middle line:84%
that A is a finite
rank operator.

00:55:23.913 --> 00:55:25.830 align:middle line:84%
So I could have started
this whole theorem off

00:55:25.830 --> 00:55:29.160 align:middle line:84%
with let's assume A is not
a finite rank operator,

00:55:29.160 --> 00:55:31.410 align:middle line:84%
and therefore we wouldn't
have to deal with this case.

00:55:31.410 --> 00:55:34.260 align:middle line:84%
But I just stated it
for an arbitrary A.

00:55:34.260 --> 00:55:38.850 align:middle line:84%
So there is the possibility
that that sequence stopped.

00:55:38.850 --> 00:55:43.080 align:middle line:84%
We found all of the
eigenvalues with multiplicity

00:55:43.080 --> 00:55:45.160 align:middle line:90%
in this process.

00:55:45.160 --> 00:55:48.180 align:middle line:84%
And then the theorem--
or construction

00:55:48.180 --> 00:55:50.670 align:middle line:90%
is done in that case.

00:55:50.670 --> 00:55:55.080 align:middle line:84%
And the case 2 is that it is
not a finite rank operator,

00:55:55.080 --> 00:55:57.380 align:middle line:84%
so it's not equal
to k equals 1 to n.

00:55:57.380 --> 00:56:02.790 align:middle line:90%


00:56:02.790 --> 00:56:08.640 align:middle line:84%
Now we have to show how can
we find lambda sub n plus 1

00:56:08.640 --> 00:56:11.220 align:middle line:84%
and the eigenvector
u sub n plus 1.

00:56:11.220 --> 00:56:14.060 align:middle line:90%


00:56:14.060 --> 00:56:22.160 align:middle line:84%
Let a Sub n be the operator A
minus k equals 1 to n lambda

00:56:22.160 --> 00:56:24.300 align:middle line:90%
k inner product u.

00:56:24.300 --> 00:56:35.540 align:middle line:84%
So should say Au minus
u sub k, all right.

00:56:35.540 --> 00:56:41.410 align:middle line:90%


00:56:41.410 --> 00:56:41.910 align:middle line:90%
Now.

00:56:41.910 --> 00:56:46.930 align:middle line:84%
Note since we are in case
2, this operator is nonzero.

00:56:46.930 --> 00:56:49.680 align:middle line:84%
So we're basically going to
apply the previous theorem now

00:56:49.680 --> 00:56:51.090 align:middle line:90%
to this operator.

00:56:51.090 --> 00:56:53.025 align:middle line:84%
But let me first
make a few remarks.

00:56:53.025 --> 00:56:57.310 align:middle line:90%


00:56:57.310 --> 00:57:00.925 align:middle line:90%
Then A is A sub n is a--

00:57:00.925 --> 00:57:05.417 align:middle line:90%


00:57:05.417 --> 00:57:06.750 align:middle line:90%
this is something you can check.

00:57:06.750 --> 00:57:12.740 align:middle line:84%
It's a self-adjoint
compact operator.

00:57:12.740 --> 00:57:14.440 align:middle line:90%
So why is it self adjoint?

00:57:14.440 --> 00:57:18.640 align:middle line:84%
Basically because A is self
adjoint and these lambda k's

00:57:18.640 --> 00:57:23.610 align:middle line:84%
are real numbers
because eigenvalues

00:57:23.610 --> 00:57:27.820 align:middle line:84%
have to be real, because
A is self adjoint

00:57:27.820 --> 00:57:29.480 align:middle line:90%
and these are orthonormal.

00:57:29.480 --> 00:57:33.670 align:middle line:84%
So that is why
they're self adjoint.

00:57:33.670 --> 00:57:35.140 align:middle line:90%
Why is it a compact operator?

00:57:35.140 --> 00:57:39.100 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's the sum of a compact
operator A and a finite rank

00:57:39.100 --> 00:57:40.220 align:middle line:90%
operator here.

00:57:40.220 --> 00:57:44.090 align:middle line:90%
So it's also a compact operator.

00:57:44.090 --> 00:57:46.550 align:middle line:84%
And it's a nontrivial
one because it's not

00:57:46.550 --> 00:57:49.960 align:middle line:90%
identically equal to 0.

00:57:49.960 --> 00:57:52.300 align:middle line:84%
So I shouldn't say
not equal to 0,

00:57:52.300 --> 00:57:57.070 align:middle line:84%
but which is not identically
the zero operator.

00:57:57.070 --> 00:58:03.950 align:middle line:90%


00:58:03.950 --> 00:58:12.605 align:middle line:84%
So here's a couple of facts
that if u is in the span of u1

00:58:12.605 --> 00:58:21.780 align:middle line:84%
up to un, then I get
that A sub n applied to u

00:58:21.780 --> 00:58:24.630 align:middle line:90%
is the zero vector.

00:58:24.630 --> 00:58:25.410 align:middle line:90%
Why is that?

00:58:25.410 --> 00:58:29.070 align:middle line:84%
So it suffices to check
that this gives me--

00:58:29.070 --> 00:58:32.430 align:middle line:84%
this formula gives me 0
when u is one of the uk's.

00:58:32.430 --> 00:58:39.010 align:middle line:84%
Now if u is one of the uk's,
then this will be 1 only when--

00:58:39.010 --> 00:58:41.050 align:middle line:84%
or let's say it's
one of the uj's.

00:58:41.050 --> 00:58:42.500 align:middle line:90%
I need a different letter.

00:58:42.500 --> 00:58:47.410 align:middle line:84%
So if u is one of
these uj's, then this

00:58:47.410 --> 00:58:50.530 align:middle line:84%
will give me 1 only
when j equals k

00:58:50.530 --> 00:58:53.140 align:middle line:90%
and I pick up lambda j times uj.

00:58:53.140 --> 00:58:56.440 align:middle line:84%
And then I have A
hitting u sub j.

00:58:56.440 --> 00:58:58.450 align:middle line:84%
And that spits
out lambda j times

00:58:58.450 --> 00:59:01.330 align:middle line:84%
u sub j because u sub j
is an eigenvector of A.

00:59:01.330 --> 00:59:03.280 align:middle line:84%
So then I get the same
thing here and there

00:59:03.280 --> 00:59:04.765 align:middle line:90%
and subtracting them gives me 0.

00:59:04.765 --> 00:59:08.670 align:middle line:90%


00:59:08.670 --> 00:59:16.930 align:middle line:84%
Now, if u is orthogonal to
the span of the u1 up to un,

00:59:16.930 --> 00:59:23.760 align:middle line:90%
then I get A n of u equals Au.

00:59:23.760 --> 00:59:24.930 align:middle line:90%
So you just see this.

00:59:24.930 --> 00:59:29.120 align:middle line:84%
If u is orthogonal to these,
then this whole term is 0.

00:59:29.120 --> 00:59:31.250 align:middle line:84%
Can't I just pick
up An u equals Au.

00:59:31.250 --> 00:59:44.250 align:middle line:90%


00:59:44.250 --> 00:59:55.070 align:middle line:84%
For all u in H, in the
span of u1 up to un,

00:59:55.070 --> 01:00:00.980 align:middle line:84%
if I compute A and
u inner product v,

01:00:00.980 --> 01:00:07.190 align:middle line:84%
this is equal to u and v
since a n is self adjoint.

01:00:07.190 --> 01:00:09.500 align:middle line:84%
And since v is in
the span of the u1's

01:00:09.500 --> 01:00:13.160 align:middle line:84%
up to un's, by the first
property that's going to be 0.

01:00:13.160 --> 01:00:19.085 align:middle line:84%
So this equals 0 and
therefore, what have I shown?

01:00:19.085 --> 01:00:23.360 align:middle line:84%
I've shown that A and u inner
product v is 0 no matter

01:00:23.360 --> 01:00:26.810 align:middle line:84%
what u is in H.
And therefore, that

01:00:26.810 --> 01:00:30.770 align:middle line:84%
means that v has to be in
the orthogonal complement

01:00:30.770 --> 01:00:31.355 align:middle line:90%
of the range.

01:00:31.355 --> 01:00:36.638 align:middle line:90%


01:00:36.638 --> 01:00:37.680 align:middle line:90%
So I said that backwards.

01:00:37.680 --> 01:00:43.690 align:middle line:84%
Anyways, here u is fixed. v
is a thing that's changing.

01:00:43.690 --> 01:00:44.730 align:middle line:90%
So what does this say?

01:00:44.730 --> 01:00:45.960 align:middle line:90%
u as fixed.

01:00:45.960 --> 01:00:49.500 align:middle line:84%
A and u inner product v when
v is in the span has to be 0.

01:00:49.500 --> 01:00:52.020 align:middle line:84%
This holds for
all v in the span.

01:00:52.020 --> 01:00:55.260 align:middle line:84%
And therefore A n u has to be
in the orthogonal complement

01:00:55.260 --> 01:00:57.310 align:middle line:90%
of the span of these guys.

01:00:57.310 --> 01:01:03.990 align:middle line:84%
So this proves that
the range of A n

01:01:03.990 --> 01:01:13.110 align:middle line:84%
is a subset of your orthogonal
complement of u1 up to un.

01:01:13.110 --> 01:01:21.510 align:middle line:90%


01:01:21.510 --> 01:01:24.420 align:middle line:84%
And so from this
previous property

01:01:24.420 --> 01:01:33.510 align:middle line:84%
we get one last property,
that if a n u equals

01:01:33.510 --> 01:01:37.050 align:middle line:84%
lambda u is nonzero,
meaning we have

01:01:37.050 --> 01:01:45.525 align:middle line:84%
a nonzero eigenvalue of A sub
n, then that implies that u--

01:01:45.525 --> 01:01:50.240 align:middle line:90%


01:01:50.240 --> 01:01:55.140 align:middle line:84%
so u is equal to 1 over
lambda A sub n applied to u.

01:01:55.140 --> 01:01:58.500 align:middle line:84%
In other words, u is equal to A
sub n applied to u over lambda.

01:01:58.500 --> 01:02:05.180 align:middle line:84%
So that implies u is in the
range of A sub n, which again

01:02:05.180 --> 01:02:11.960 align:middle line:84%
is contained in the orthogonal
complement of u1 up to u sub n.

01:02:11.960 --> 01:02:13.910 align:middle line:84%
And since it's in the
orthogonal complement,

01:02:13.910 --> 01:02:16.270 align:middle line:90%
that means A sub nu equals Au.

01:02:16.270 --> 01:02:29.151 align:middle line:90%


01:02:29.151 --> 01:02:33.000 align:middle line:84%
So any nonzero
eigenvalue of A sub n

01:02:33.000 --> 01:02:43.070 align:middle line:84%
has to be a nonzero
eigenvalue of A.

01:02:43.070 --> 01:02:50.250 align:middle line:84%
Now we just apply
the previous theorem

01:02:50.250 --> 01:03:00.140 align:middle line:84%
to A sub n to the next
eigenvalue lambda sub n plus 1

01:03:00.140 --> 01:03:02.450 align:middle line:90%
and eigenvector u sub n plus 1.

01:03:02.450 --> 01:03:10.559 align:middle line:90%


01:03:10.559 --> 01:03:28.880 align:middle line:84%
By the previous theorem, a
sub n as a nonzero eigenvalue,

01:03:28.880 --> 01:03:42.260 align:middle line:84%
which I will call lambda n
plus 1, with unit eigenvector

01:03:42.260 --> 01:03:50.020 align:middle line:84%
u sub n plus 1 so
that lambda n plus 1

01:03:50.020 --> 01:03:57.460 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the
sup over all norm--

01:03:57.460 --> 01:04:11.540 align:middle line:90%


01:04:11.540 --> 01:04:16.510 align:middle line:84%
ah, OK, sup over all norm
or unit length vectors

01:04:16.510 --> 01:04:21.010 align:middle line:84%
of absolute value of A sub n
applied to u, inner product u.

01:04:21.010 --> 01:04:30.350 align:middle line:84%
Now, thos sup is the
same sup over all 1

01:04:30.350 --> 01:04:32.760 align:middle line:90%
so that A sub n u is nonzero.

01:04:32.760 --> 01:04:35.270 align:middle line:90%
So that in particular includes--

01:04:35.270 --> 01:04:51.700 align:middle line:90%


01:04:51.700 --> 01:04:52.200 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:04:52.200 --> 01:04:59.030 align:middle line:90%


01:04:59.030 --> 01:05:04.430 align:middle line:84%
So if u is in the span u1 up
to un, then A and u equals 0.

01:05:04.430 --> 01:05:08.810 align:middle line:84%
So the supremum over all unit
length is the same as u--

01:05:08.810 --> 01:05:10.910 align:middle line:84%
the supremum over
everything in the orthogonal

01:05:10.910 --> 01:05:13.610 align:middle line:84%
complement of these
guys because these guys,

01:05:13.610 --> 01:05:16.730 align:middle line:84%
when you stick them into
A, and gives me a 0.

01:05:16.730 --> 01:05:18.770 align:middle line:90%
So that's the same supremum.

01:05:18.770 --> 01:05:23.480 align:middle line:84%
But now also when u is in the
orthogonal complement of these

01:05:23.480 --> 01:05:41.490 align:middle line:84%
u1's up to un, this is equal
to sup of u on the orthogonal

01:05:41.490 --> 01:05:43.380 align:middle line:90%
complement of the u1 up to un's.

01:05:43.380 --> 01:05:46.702 align:middle line:90%
A n u, remember, equals Au.

01:05:46.702 --> 01:05:50.530 align:middle line:90%


01:05:50.530 --> 01:05:57.160 align:middle line:84%
So that gives us the
fact that I can find this

01:05:57.160 --> 01:05:59.695 align:middle line:90%
from this supremum.

01:05:59.695 --> 01:06:03.150 align:middle line:90%


01:06:03.150 --> 01:06:10.560 align:middle line:84%
And so why does
this eigenvector has

01:06:10.560 --> 01:06:19.690 align:middle line:84%
to be in the orthogonal
complement of u1 up to un?

01:06:19.690 --> 01:06:20.190 align:middle line:90%
it's.

01:06:20.190 --> 01:06:24.300 align:middle line:84%
Because I can choose it that
way because when A sub n hits

01:06:24.300 --> 01:06:27.210 align:middle line:90%
anything in here, I get 0.

01:06:27.210 --> 01:06:34.220 align:middle line:90%


01:06:34.220 --> 01:06:37.700 align:middle line:84%
So I should say this
is also equal to A n

01:06:37.700 --> 01:06:39.440 align:middle line:90%
apply to un plus 1.

01:06:39.440 --> 01:06:43.940 align:middle line:84%
But that's the same as
A applied to un plus 1--

01:06:43.940 --> 01:06:44.840 align:middle line:90%
un plus 1.

01:06:44.840 --> 01:06:51.690 align:middle line:90%


01:06:51.690 --> 01:06:56.220 align:middle line:84%
And this is less than or equal
to the sup over norm u equals

01:06:56.220 --> 01:07:06.530 align:middle line:84%
1 u in span u1 up to un minus
1, orthogonal complement

01:07:06.530 --> 01:07:13.520 align:middle line:84%
Au applied to u, which
equals the absolute value

01:07:13.520 --> 01:07:18.140 align:middle line:84%
of the n-th eigenvalue
counted with multiplicity.

01:07:18.140 --> 01:07:25.760 align:middle line:84%
So we found the next
eigenvalue in this sequence

01:07:25.760 --> 01:07:28.198 align:middle line:84%
of eigenvalues
countable multiplicity.

01:07:28.198 --> 01:07:41.380 align:middle line:90%


01:07:41.380 --> 01:07:50.140 align:middle line:84%
So now, we can conclude the
following spectral theorem

01:07:50.140 --> 01:07:52.750 align:middle line:84%
for compact
self-adjoint operators.

01:07:52.750 --> 01:07:59.360 align:middle line:90%


01:07:59.360 --> 01:08:23.859 align:middle line:84%
Let A be a self-adjoint compact
operator on a separable Hilbert

01:08:23.859 --> 01:08:31.560 align:middle line:90%
space H.

01:08:31.560 --> 01:08:39.138 align:middle line:84%
Let lambda 1 bigger than
or equal to lambda 2

01:08:39.138 --> 01:08:47.839 align:middle line:84%
be corresponding
eigenvalues, be the--

01:08:47.839 --> 01:08:57.340 align:middle line:90%


01:08:57.340 --> 01:09:00.819 align:middle line:84%
eigenvalues-- or I should
say nonzero eigenvalues--

01:09:00.819 --> 01:09:06.420 align:middle line:90%


01:09:06.420 --> 01:09:17.410 align:middle line:84%
of A counted again with
multiplicity, so counted

01:09:17.410 --> 01:09:19.779 align:middle line:84%
with multiplicity,
as we've constructed

01:09:19.779 --> 01:09:26.649 align:middle line:84%
in this theorem which I
called the maximum principle,

01:09:26.649 --> 01:09:45.910 align:middle line:84%
with corresponding
orthonormal eigenvectors uk.

01:09:45.910 --> 01:09:56.050 align:middle line:84%
So we have these eigenvectors
coming from this process.

01:09:56.050 --> 01:10:12.310 align:middle line:84%
And the conclusion is this
subset of eigenvectors,

01:10:12.310 --> 01:10:22.040 align:middle line:84%
or the orthonormal eigenvectors
is an orthonormal basis

01:10:22.040 --> 01:10:27.240 align:middle line:90%
for the closure--

01:10:27.240 --> 01:10:31.390 align:middle line:84%
or for the range
of A. In fact, we

01:10:31.390 --> 01:10:40.900 align:middle line:84%
can upgrade that the uk is,
in fact, an orthonormal basis

01:10:40.900 --> 01:10:46.810 align:middle line:90%
for the range of A closure.

01:10:46.810 --> 01:11:02.530 align:middle line:84%
And there exists an orthonormal
basis, call it f sub j,

01:11:02.530 --> 01:11:20.795 align:middle line:84%
of the null space of A if it's
nonzero, such that uk k fj.

01:11:20.795 --> 01:11:23.590 align:middle line:90%


01:11:23.590 --> 01:11:28.460 align:middle line:84%
So, first off, the
union of these two sets

01:11:28.460 --> 01:11:30.500 align:middle line:84%
of orthonormal
vectors is then going

01:11:30.500 --> 01:11:33.410 align:middle line:84%
to be again a subset
of orthonormal vectors

01:11:33.410 --> 01:11:39.740 align:middle line:84%
because all the fj's would
correspond to the eigenvalue 0.

01:11:39.740 --> 01:11:43.340 align:middle line:84%
And these uk's would
correspond to eigenvalues

01:11:43.340 --> 01:11:45.350 align:middle line:90%
that are nonzero.

01:11:45.350 --> 01:11:49.490 align:middle line:84%
And by what we proved
last time, any eigenvector

01:11:49.490 --> 01:11:52.800 align:middle line:84%
for two distinct eigenvalues
would have to be orthonormal.

01:11:52.800 --> 01:11:57.200 align:middle line:84%
So this subset is
orthonormal from this subset.

01:11:57.200 --> 01:12:03.710 align:middle line:84%
But, moreover, is orthonormal
like I said, but also

01:12:03.710 --> 01:12:10.200 align:middle line:90%
an orthonormal basis for H.

01:12:10.200 --> 01:12:15.150 align:middle line:84%
So, in other words, I can
find an orthonormal basis

01:12:15.150 --> 01:12:19.320 align:middle line:84%
for H consisting
entirely of eigenvectors

01:12:19.320 --> 01:12:27.270 align:middle line:84%
of this self-adjoint
compact operator, OK.

01:12:27.270 --> 01:12:30.150 align:middle line:84%
So I will have one piece
of this basis coming

01:12:30.150 --> 01:12:34.380 align:middle line:84%
from the null space and the
other piece corresponding

01:12:34.380 --> 01:12:37.560 align:middle line:90%
to nonzero eigenvalues.

01:12:37.560 --> 01:12:40.410 align:middle line:90%


01:12:40.410 --> 01:12:42.905 align:middle line:90%
So, really, 2 follows from 1.

01:12:42.905 --> 01:12:49.830 align:middle line:90%


01:12:49.830 --> 01:12:52.330 align:middle line:84%
Not sure why I decided
to state them separately,

01:12:52.330 --> 01:12:53.185 align:middle line:90%
but here we are.

01:12:53.185 --> 01:12:58.240 align:middle line:90%


01:12:58.240 --> 01:13:01.600 align:middle line:84%
So proof of 2 will show that
uk is in orthonormal basis

01:13:01.600 --> 01:13:04.300 align:middle line:90%
for the range of A.

01:13:04.300 --> 01:13:07.420 align:middle line:84%
So first off note as we
did in the previous proof

01:13:07.420 --> 01:13:18.460 align:middle line:84%
that the process of
obtaining the lambda

01:13:18.460 --> 01:13:33.450 align:middle line:84%
1 for the eigenvalues
and eigenvectors,

01:13:33.450 --> 01:13:43.390 align:middle line:84%
or orthonormal eigenvectors
terminates if and only

01:13:43.390 --> 01:13:50.120 align:middle line:90%
if A was finite rank.

01:13:50.120 --> 01:13:51.740 align:middle line:84%
In other words,
their exists an n

01:13:51.740 --> 01:14:07.405 align:middle line:84%
so that Au is this finite rink
operator u in a product uk, uk.

01:14:07.405 --> 01:14:18.720 align:middle line:90%


01:14:18.720 --> 01:14:20.790 align:middle line:84%
In this case, if it's
a finite rank operator,

01:14:20.790 --> 01:14:28.520 align:middle line:84%
then the range is
contained in uk, uk,

01:14:28.520 --> 01:14:37.670 align:middle line:84%
which is what we wanted
to prove for this case,

01:14:37.670 --> 01:14:40.960 align:middle line:84%
that A is this
finite rank operator.

01:14:40.960 --> 01:14:47.390 align:middle line:84%
So suppose otherwise,
in other words

01:14:47.390 --> 01:14:55.440 align:middle line:84%
the process does not
terminate, so that we

01:14:55.440 --> 01:15:01.520 align:middle line:84%
have countably infinite many
nonzero eigenvalues counted

01:15:01.520 --> 01:15:05.810 align:middle line:84%
with multiplicity and
corresponding orthonormal

01:15:05.810 --> 01:15:07.490 align:middle line:90%
eigenvectors u sub k.

01:15:07.490 --> 01:15:10.540 align:middle line:90%
So this is a more interesting--

01:15:10.540 --> 01:15:24.320 align:middle line:90%


01:15:24.320 --> 01:15:26.560 align:middle line:84%
so the eigenvalues are
countably infinite.

01:15:26.560 --> 01:15:33.280 align:middle line:90%


01:15:33.280 --> 01:15:39.260 align:middle line:84%
OK, so now we want to
show-- so as in the remark

01:15:39.260 --> 01:15:44.720 align:middle line:84%
that I made afterwards, you
know that these lambda k's

01:15:44.720 --> 01:15:47.240 align:middle line:90%
have to go to 0.

01:15:47.240 --> 01:15:51.840 align:middle line:84%
Now, we want to
prove that the uk's

01:15:51.840 --> 01:15:56.535 align:middle line:84%
are an orthonormal basis
for the range of A.

01:15:56.535 --> 01:15:57.540 align:middle line:90%
What does that mean?

01:15:57.540 --> 01:15:59.250 align:middle line:84%
By definition, that
means that they're

01:15:59.250 --> 01:16:02.160 align:middle line:84%
in maximal orthonormal
subset of the range of.

01:16:02.160 --> 01:16:04.650 align:middle line:84%
A So we have to show that
if something's in the range

01:16:04.650 --> 01:16:08.940 align:middle line:84%
and it's orthogonal to every
one of these eigenvectors,

01:16:08.940 --> 01:16:10.440 align:middle line:90%
then that thing has to be 0.

01:16:10.440 --> 01:16:14.880 align:middle line:90%


01:16:14.880 --> 01:16:26.338 align:middle line:84%
So the claim if f is in the
range of A, and for all k

01:16:26.338 --> 01:16:32.830 align:middle line:84%
f inner product uk equals
0, then f is a 0 vector.

01:16:32.830 --> 01:16:44.047 align:middle line:90%


01:16:44.047 --> 01:16:45.630 align:middle line:84%
So this is the claim
we want to prove.

01:16:45.630 --> 01:16:47.460 align:middle line:84%
So suppose we have
something in the range.

01:16:47.460 --> 01:16:50.760 align:middle line:84%
That means we can
write it as A times u

01:16:50.760 --> 01:16:57.090 align:middle line:84%
and f inner product
uk equals 0 for all k.

01:16:57.090 --> 01:17:08.170 align:middle line:90%


01:17:08.170 --> 01:17:16.490 align:middle line:84%
Then for all k, if I look at
lambda ku inner product uk,

01:17:16.490 --> 01:17:19.880 align:middle line:90%
this is equal to--

01:17:19.880 --> 01:17:22.340 align:middle line:84%
lambda k is a real
number so I can bring it

01:17:22.340 --> 01:17:26.060 align:middle line:84%
all the way in,
get lambda k, uk.

01:17:26.060 --> 01:17:31.580 align:middle line:84%
And this is equal
to u A apply to uk.

01:17:31.580 --> 01:17:35.360 align:middle line:84%
Now, a is self adjoint so I
can move this a over here to u.

01:17:35.360 --> 01:17:38.330 align:middle line:90%


01:17:38.330 --> 01:17:43.520 align:middle line:84%
And this is equal to f inner
product uk equals 0 for all k.

01:17:43.520 --> 01:17:54.228 align:middle line:90%


01:17:54.228 --> 01:18:02.720 align:middle line:84%
So by this maximum
principle which

01:18:02.720 --> 01:18:06.640 align:middle line:84%
we proved just a
minute ago we conclude

01:18:06.640 --> 01:18:13.480 align:middle line:84%
that the norm of f, which is
equal to the norm of Au, which

01:18:13.480 --> 01:18:21.620 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the norm of A
minus sum from k equals 1 to n

01:18:21.620 --> 01:18:33.270 align:middle line:84%
of lambda k u, uk,
uk applied to u

01:18:33.270 --> 01:18:35.310 align:middle line:84%
because every one of
these numbers is 0,

01:18:35.310 --> 01:18:38.145 align:middle line:84%
so I haven't subtracted
off anything.

01:18:38.145 --> 01:18:47.060 align:middle line:90%


01:18:47.060 --> 01:18:55.040 align:middle line:84%
So I can write this in terms
of this A sub n applied to u,

01:18:55.040 --> 01:18:58.070 align:middle line:90%
where A sub n was this thing--

01:18:58.070 --> 01:19:03.530 align:middle line:84%
or A sub n is this
thing, which by the proof

01:19:03.530 --> 01:19:06.230 align:middle line:84%
of the maximization--
or the maximum principle

01:19:06.230 --> 01:19:13.720 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to
lambda plus 1 n plus 1 of u

01:19:13.720 --> 01:19:20.580 align:middle line:84%
because, again,
remember this thing here

01:19:20.580 --> 01:19:23.670 align:middle line:84%
is the supremum over
all u's of unit length

01:19:23.670 --> 01:19:25.360 align:middle line:90%
of A sub n applied to u.

01:19:25.360 --> 01:19:28.920 align:middle line:84%
So lambda n plus
1 is less than--

01:19:28.920 --> 01:19:32.220 align:middle line:84%
or this quantity here
divided by the norm of u

01:19:32.220 --> 01:19:34.740 align:middle line:84%
so that you as a unit
length is always less than

01:19:34.740 --> 01:19:36.690 align:middle line:90%
or equal to this quantity here.

01:19:36.690 --> 01:19:38.910 align:middle line:90%
But now lambda-- so this--

01:19:38.910 --> 01:19:41.340 align:middle line:84%
I had a fixed thing
here, norm of f.

01:19:41.340 --> 01:19:43.560 align:middle line:84%
And I've shown it's less
than or equal to lambda n

01:19:43.560 --> 01:19:45.330 align:middle line:90%
plus 1 times the norm of u.

01:19:45.330 --> 01:19:46.650 align:middle line:90%
This is a fixed thing.

01:19:46.650 --> 01:19:48.570 align:middle line:84%
And the lambda n's
are converging to 0.

01:19:48.570 --> 01:19:53.140 align:middle line:90%


01:19:53.140 --> 01:19:56.950 align:middle line:84%
And thus I started off with
something non-negative,

01:19:56.950 --> 01:19:59.410 align:middle line:84%
less than or equal to
something converging to 0.

01:19:59.410 --> 01:20:04.690 align:middle line:84%
And therefore that
thing had to be 0.

01:20:04.690 --> 01:20:06.430 align:middle line:90%
Therefore f is 0.

01:20:06.430 --> 01:20:11.350 align:middle line:84%
So this proves, 1,
that these eigenvectors

01:20:11.350 --> 01:20:24.305 align:middle line:84%
are a maximal orthonormal
subset of the range of A.

01:20:24.305 --> 01:20:35.810 align:middle line:84%
And for 2 we simply
note that by 1, we have

01:20:35.810 --> 01:20:41.250 align:middle line:84%
that the range of
A closure, this

01:20:41.250 --> 01:20:45.300 align:middle line:84%
is since the eigenvectors
are in orthonormal basis

01:20:45.300 --> 01:20:53.230 align:middle line:84%
for the range of A, the
closure is contained in--

01:20:53.230 --> 01:21:01.770 align:middle line:90%


01:21:01.770 --> 01:21:05.580 align:middle line:84%
closure of the spans
of the uk's, here this

01:21:05.580 --> 01:21:13.440 align:middle line:84%
is a finite span,
which, remember,

01:21:13.440 --> 01:21:16.350 align:middle line:84%
this is by an
assignment exercise,

01:21:16.350 --> 01:21:20.850 align:middle line:84%
this is equal to k
ck, uk such that--

01:21:20.850 --> 01:21:27.030 align:middle line:90%


01:21:27.030 --> 01:21:39.840 align:middle line:84%
and therefore, this implies
that is an orthonormal basis

01:21:39.840 --> 01:21:45.210 align:middle line:90%
for the range of A closure.

01:21:45.210 --> 01:21:56.820 align:middle line:84%
And this is equal to the range
of A orthogonal complement,

01:21:56.820 --> 01:22:06.810 align:middle line:84%
orthogonal complement, which
is equal to the null space of--

01:22:06.810 --> 01:22:09.850 align:middle line:84%
or the orthogonal complement
of the null space of A star.

01:22:09.850 --> 01:22:11.600 align:middle line:90%
A star equals A, so we get that.

01:22:11.600 --> 01:22:17.440 align:middle line:84%
So the uk's-- the eigenvectors
form an orthonormal basis

01:22:17.440 --> 01:22:20.710 align:middle line:84%
for the orthogonal complement
of the null space of A.

01:22:20.710 --> 01:22:26.110 align:middle line:84%
So once we've chosen orthonormal
basis for null space of A,

01:22:26.110 --> 01:22:28.658 align:middle line:90%
that's it.

01:22:28.658 --> 01:22:33.700 align:middle line:84%
Since H is separable
and the null space of A

01:22:33.700 --> 01:22:45.250 align:middle line:84%
is a closed subspace of H,
null space of A inseparable.

01:22:45.250 --> 01:22:48.580 align:middle line:84%
And we've proven that
every separable Hilbert

01:22:48.580 --> 01:22:52.780 align:middle line:90%
space, or even just a--

01:22:52.780 --> 01:22:56.050 align:middle line:84%
so this is a close one so
it's a Hilbert space anyways.

01:22:56.050 --> 01:22:57.490 align:middle line:84%
But every separable
Hilbert space

01:22:57.490 --> 01:23:06.000 align:middle line:90%
has an orthonormal basis of--

01:23:06.000 --> 01:23:10.940 align:middle line:90%


01:23:10.940 --> 01:23:17.010 align:middle line:84%
and therefore, again,
so since these two--

01:23:17.010 --> 01:23:31.400 align:middle line:84%
call this fj-- this is
an orthonormal basis

01:23:31.400 --> 01:23:38.480 align:middle line:84%
for the null space of A
direct product null space

01:23:38.480 --> 01:23:41.990 align:middle line:90%
of A orthogonal complement.

01:23:41.990 --> 01:23:44.630 align:middle line:90%
That equals H.

01:23:44.630 --> 01:23:47.790 align:middle line:84%
And just in the nick
of time I'm finished.

01:23:47.790 --> 01:23:52.210 align:middle line:84%
So next time where
do we go from here?

01:23:52.210 --> 01:23:56.550 align:middle line:84%
We'll see some of this
applied in a concrete setting

01:23:56.550 --> 01:23:58.590 align:middle line:84%
of differentiable
equations and also discuss

01:23:58.590 --> 01:24:00.680 align:middle line:90%
the functional calculus.

01:24:00.680 --> 01:24:06.000 align:middle line:90%